<urn:cpi:source:15:94> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 94" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1. Purpose
This clause, titled '3.1 DSCR Minimums,' is a synthetic demonstration document designed to outline the minimum Debt Service
Coverage Ratio (DSCR) requirements for retail credit products under the parent policy CP-Retail-v3.1. The purpose of this
clause is to establish clear, consistent, and risk-based thresholds for DSCR to ensure the financial stability of both the borrower
and the lending institution. By defining these minimums, the bank aims to mitigate credit risk, align with regulatory expectations,
and maintain portfolio quality.
The DSCR is a critical financial metric used to assess a borrower's ability to generate sufficient cash flow to cover their debt
obligations. This clause ensures that all retail credit products adhere to a standardized approach for evaluating DSCR, thereby
promoting uniformity in underwriting decisions. The synthetic nature of this document allows for the illustration of best practices
without referencing real-world data or proprietary information.
This clause is intended for use by underwriters, credit analysts, risk managers, and other stakeholders involved in the retail
credit approval process. It provides a framework for evaluating DSCR in a manner that is both transparent and reproducible,
ensuring that all decisions are grounded in objective criteria. The policy also serves as a reference for auditors and regulators to
verify compliance with internal and external standards.
The establishment of DSCR minimums is particularly important in retail lending, where borrowers may have varying levels of
financial sophistication. By setting clear thresholds, the bank can ensure that borrowers are not overleveraged, thereby
reducing the likelihood of default. This clause also supports the bank's broader risk management objectives by aligning DSCR
requirements with the bank's risk appetite and capital adequacy goals.
This synthetic policy is designed to be internally consistent with the parent policy CP-Retail-v3.1 and other related credit
policies. It is not based on real data but is crafted to demonstrate how a bank might structure its DSCR requirements to balance
risk and opportunity. All examples, thresholds, and roles referenced herein are fictional and for illustrative purposes only."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

