<urn:cpi:source:21:119> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 119" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1. Purpose
The purpose of this clause, 3.2 LTV Ceilings, under the parent policy CP-Retail-v3.1, is to establish clear, consistent, and
risk-based Loan-to-Value (LTV) ceilings for all retail lending products offered by the bank. This clause ensures that the bank
maintains a prudent balance between risk exposure and customer accessibility, aligning with regulatory requirements and
internal risk appetite frameworks. By defining maximum LTV ratios, the bank aims to mitigate potential losses arising from
collateral depreciation or default scenarios while fostering responsible lending practices.
This synthetic demonstration document is designed to illustrate how LTV ceilings are structured within a broader credit policy
framework. It provides a realistic example of how thresholds, roles, and controls are integrated to create a robust underwriting
process. While the data and scenarios presented are fictional, they are internally consistent and reflect industry best practices
for retail lending.
The LTV ceilings outlined herein apply to all secured retail lending products, including mortgages, auto loans, and personal
loans backed by collateral. The policy ensures that the bank does not overextend credit relative to the value of the underlying
asset, thereby protecting both the bank and the borrower from excessive risk. This is particularly critical in economic downturns
or market volatility, where asset values may fluctuate significantly.
This clause also serves as a reference for underwriters, risk managers, and compliance teams to ensure uniform application of
LTV limits across all retail lending portfolios. By standardizing these limits, the bank can maintain consistency in
decision-making, reduce discretionary variances, and enhance the overall quality of its loan portfolio.
Finally, the purpose of this clause extends to supporting the bank’s strategic objectives, such as sustainable growth and
customer trust. By adhering to well-defined LTV ceilings, the bank demonstrates its commitment to prudent lending, which in
turn strengthens its reputation and stability in the marketplace."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

