<urn:cpi:source:29:150> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 150" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 1.1.3 Governance and Accountability
Governance of the enterprise limits is the responsibility of the Credit Policy Committee (CPC), which is chaired by the Chief
Risk Officer (CRO) and includes representation from Business Line Heads, the Chief Financial Officer (CFO), the Chief
Compliance Officer (CCO), and the Head of Internal Audit. The CPC is empowered to set, review, and adjust enterprise limits,
as well as to approve exceptions and waivers in accordance with the escalation paths defined in Section 1.1.7.
The CPC meets quarterly to review the effectiveness of the enterprise limits framework, with ad-hoc meetings convened as
necessary to address emerging risks or material breaches. Minutes of all CPC meetings are documented and retained for a
minimum of seven years, in accordance with the institution’s record-keeping policy. Decisions of the CPC are binding and must
be implemented by all business lines within 10 business days of communication.
Accountability for adherence to enterprise limits rests with the Business Line Heads, who are responsible for ensuring that their
respective portfolios remain within approved thresholds. Business Line Heads must designate a Credit Risk Officer (CRO) for
their line, who acts as the primary point of contact for limit monitoring and reporting. The CRO is responsible for escalating
potential or actual breaches to the CPC within 24 hours of identification.
The Head of Credit Risk Management (HCRM) is responsible for the day-to-day administration of the enterprise limits, including
the development of methodologies, the calculation of exposures, and the production of limit utilization reports. The HCRM
reports directly to the CRO and provides a monthly dashboard to the CPC, highlighting trends, breaches, and emerging risks.
The dashboard includes synthetic data to demonstrate the institution’s ability to monitor and manage limits effectively.
Internal Audit conducts an independent review of the enterprise limits framework at least annually, with the findings presented
to the CPC and the Board Risk Committee. The audit scope includes an assessment of the design and operating effectiveness
of controls, as well as compliance with the policy’s requirements. Any deficiencies identified are tracked to remediation, with the
HCRM responsible for implementing corrective actions."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:151> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 151" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
