<urn:cpi:source:29:152> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 152" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 1.1.4 Underwriting Criteria and Risk Assessment
Underwriting criteria for credit exposures subject to enterprise limits are established to ensure that all transactions are
consistent with the institution’s risk appetite and credit standards. The criteria include quantitative and qualitative factors, such
as the counterparty’s credit rating, financial strength, industry outlook, and management quality. Synthetic thresholds for these
criteria are defined in the Underwriting Standards Manual, which is maintained by the HCRM and updated annually.
For corporate exposures, the minimum credit rating threshold for new transactions is ‘BBB-’ or equivalent, as assessed by the
institution’s internal rating system or a major external credit rating agency. Exposures to counterparties rated below this
threshold require approval from the CPC, with additional due diligence and enhanced monitoring. Retail and commercial
exposures are subject to separate underwriting criteria, as outlined in the Retail Credit Policy and Commercial Credit Policy,
respectively.
The risk assessment process for enterprise limits includes a forward-looking evaluation of the counterparty’s ability to meet its
obligations under stressed scenarios. Stress testing is conducted at least annually, using scenarios defined by the institution’s
Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) framework. The results of stress tests are used to inform limit-setting and to identify
potential concentrations or vulnerabilities in the portfolio.
All credit exposures must be supported by a comprehensive credit analysis, which includes a detailed financial review, industry
analysis, and assessment of collateral or guarantees, where applicable. The credit analysis must be documented in the
institution’s credit file system and approved by the designated credit authority, as defined in the Delegated Authority Matrix. The
credit file must be updated at least annually, or more frequently if there are material changes in the counterparty’s risk profile.
The underwriting criteria and risk assessment methodologies are subject to periodic validation by the Model Risk Management
(MRM) team. The MRM team reviews the performance of internal rating models, the accuracy of risk parameter estimates, and
the effectiveness of stress testing scenarios. Findings from these validations are reported to the CPC and incorporated into the
policy framework as necessary."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:153> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 153" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
