<urn:cpi:source:29:154> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 154" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 1.1.5 Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
Quantitative limits under this clause are designed to cap the institution’s aggregate credit exposure to individual counterparties,
sectors, geographies, and product types. The limits are expressed as a percentage of Tier 1 capital, total risk-weighted assets
(RWA), or other relevant metrics, as specified in the Limit Framework Document. Synthetic thresholds are set at conservative
levels to demonstrate prudent risk management and are reviewed at least annually by the CPC.
The primary quantitative limits include: (1) Single Counterparty Limit: 15% of Tier 1 capital for any single corporate group or
non-sovereign entity; (2) Sector Limit: 25% of total RWA for any single economic sector, as defined by the institution’s sector
classification taxonomy; (3) Geographic Limit: 20% of total RWA for any single country or region, excluding the institution’s
home jurisdiction; and (4) Product Limit: 10% of total RWA for any single product type, such as commercial real estate or
leveraged loans.
In addition to the primary limits, the institution maintains secondary thresholds to provide early warning of potential breaches.
These include: (1) Utilization Alert: 80% of any primary limit, triggering enhanced monitoring and reporting; (2) Breach
Threshold: 90% of any primary limit, requiring immediate escalation to the CPC; and (3) Hard Limit: 100% of any primary limit,
prohibiting new exposures until the breach is resolved or an exception is approved.
The calculation of exposure for limit monitoring purposes is performed in accordance with the institution’s Exposure
Measurement Methodology, which aligns with regulatory requirements and internal risk management standards. Exposures are
measured on a gross basis, with adjustments made for collateral, guarantees, and netting agreements, where applicable. The
methodology is documented in the Exposure Measurement Policy and is subject to independent validation by the MRM team.
Limit utilization is monitored on a daily basis, with reports generated by the HCRM and distributed to the CPC, Business Line
Heads, and other relevant stakeholders. The reports include a breakdown of exposures by counterparty, sector, geography,
and product, as well as a comparison of current utilization against approved limits. Synthetic data is used in these reports to
demonstrate the institution’s ability to track and manage exposures effectively."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:155> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 155" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
