<urn:cpi:source:29:156> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 156" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 1.1.6 Approval Authorities and Delegation
Approval authorities for credit exposures subject to enterprise limits are defined in the Delegated Authority Matrix, which
specifies the monetary thresholds and risk levels that can be approved by individual roles or committees. The matrix is
maintained by the HCRM and approved by the CPC, with updates communicated to all relevant personnel. Synthetic thresholds
in the matrix are designed to reflect a hierarchical approval structure, ensuring that higher-risk or larger exposures receive
appropriate oversight.
The Delegated Authority Matrix includes the following key roles: (1) Relationship Manager: Authorized to approve exposures up
to $5 million for existing counterparties with a credit rating of ‘BBB’ or higher; (2) Credit Risk Officer (CRO): Authorized to
approve exposures up to $20 million, subject to compliance with underwriting criteria and limit utilization; (3) Business Line
Head: Authorized to approve exposures up to $50 million, with additional approval required for exposures exceeding 5% of the
relevant enterprise limit; and (4) Credit Policy Committee (CPC): Required for exposures exceeding $50 million or 10% of the
relevant enterprise limit.
Delegation of authority is based on the principle of segregation of duties, ensuring that no single individual has the ability to
approve, monitor, and report on the same exposure. All approvals must be documented in the institution’s credit approval
system, with a clear audit trail of the decision-making process, including the rationale for approval, any conditions or covenants
imposed, and the identity of the approving authority.
In cases where an exposure requires approval from multiple authorities (e.g., due to its size, risk level, or limit utilization), the
approvals must be obtained sequentially, starting with the lowest authority level. The final approval must be obtained before the
exposure is booked, and all conditions or covenants must be satisfied prior to disbursement. The HCRM is responsible for
verifying compliance with approval requirements and escalating any non-compliance to the CPC.
The Delegated Authority Matrix is reviewed at least annually by the CPC, with adjustments made to reflect changes in the
institution’s risk appetite, organizational structure, or regulatory requirements. Any proposed changes to the matrix must be
accompanied by a risk assessment and business justification, and are subject to approval by the CRO. Approved changes are
communicated to all relevant personnel and incorporated into the credit approval system within 10 business days."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:157> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 157" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "RAF-2026"^^xsd:string ;
