<urn:cpi:source:29:158> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 158" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """7. 1.1.7 Monitoring and Escalation Paths
Monitoring of enterprise limits is performed on a continuous basis, with automated systems and manual processes used to track
exposure utilization against approved thresholds. The HCRM is responsible for the design and implementation of monitoring
tools, which are subject to independent validation by Internal Audit. Synthetic data is used to test the effectiveness of these
tools and ensure they can accurately identify breaches or potential breaches.
The monitoring process includes daily calculations of exposure utilization, with alerts generated for any breaches of primary or
secondary thresholds. Alerts are escalated to the relevant Business Line Head and the HCRM, who are responsible for
investigating the cause of the breach and determining the appropriate response. Breaches of hard limits (100% utilization) must
be escalated to the CPC within 24 hours, with a remediation plan submitted within five business days.
Escalation paths are defined based on the severity and duration of the breach, as well as the risk level of the exposure. The
following escalation paths apply: (1) Utilization Alert (80% of limit): Escalated to the Business Line Head and HCRM, with a
requirement to monitor the exposure closely and report on its status at the next CPC meeting; (2) Breach Threshold (90% of
limit): Escalated to the CPC, with a requirement to submit a remediation plan within five business days; and (3) Hard Limit
(100% of limit): Escalated to the CPC and the Board Risk Committee, with a prohibition on new exposures until the breach is
resolved.
The remediation plan for a limit breach must include a detailed analysis of the cause of the breach, the actions required to bring
the exposure back within limits, and the timeline for implementation. The plan must be approved by the CPC and monitored by
the HCRM until the breach is resolved. In cases where the breach cannot be resolved within the approved timeline, the CPC
may require the disposal of assets or the implementation of additional risk mitigants.
The HCRM provides a monthly report to the CPC on the status of all limit breaches, including the root cause, remediation
actions, and current utilization levels. The report also includes an analysis of trends in limit utilization, such as increasing
concentrations or emerging risks, and recommendations for adjustments to the limit framework. The CPC uses this report to
inform its decision-making and ensure that the enterprise limits remain effective and aligned with the institution’s risk appetite."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:159> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 159" ;
