<urn:cpi:source:29:160> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 160" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 1.1.8 Exceptions and Waivers
Exceptions to the enterprise limits may be granted in limited circumstances, where the exposure is deemed to be in the best
interest of the institution and the risks are appropriately mitigated. Exceptions are approved by the CPC, with the CRO having
the authority to grant temporary exceptions for up to 30 days in emergency situations. All exceptions must be documented in
the Exceptions Register, which is maintained by the HCRM and reviewed by the CPC at each meeting.
A request for an exception must be submitted to the HCRM and include the following information: (1) a description of the
exposure, including the counterparty, sector, geography, and product type; (2) the amount and duration of the requested
exception; (3) the rationale for the exception, including the strategic or financial benefits to the institution; (4) a risk assessment,
including the potential impact on the institution’s risk profile and the mitigants in place; and (5) the proposed monitoring and
reporting plan for the exception.
The HCRM reviews the exception request for completeness and accuracy, and may request additional information or analysis
from the Business Line Head or other stakeholders. The request is then presented to the CPC for consideration, with a
recommendation from the HCRM. The CPC evaluates the request based on the institution’s risk appetite, the potential benefits
and risks, and the effectiveness of the proposed mitigants. The decision of the CPC is final and must be implemented within five
business days of approval.
Temporary exceptions granted by the CRO in emergency situations must be ratified by the CPC at its next scheduled meeting.
The CRO must provide a written justification for the temporary exception, including the circumstances that necessitated the
urgent approval and the steps taken to mitigate the risks. The Exceptions Register is updated to reflect the temporary
exception, and the HCRM monitors the exposure closely until the CPC’s ratification.
Exceptions are subject to enhanced monitoring and reporting, with the Business Line Head required to provide monthly updates
to the HCRM on the status of the exposure, the effectiveness of the mitigants, and any changes in the risk profile. The HCRM
includes a summary of all active exceptions in the monthly limit utilization report to the CPC, with a focus on the highest-risk or
longest-duration exceptions. Exceptions that are no longer required or justified must be closed out, with the exposure brought
back within the approved limits."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:161> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
