<urn:cpi:source:29:162> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 162" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 1.1.9 Reporting and Transparency
Reporting on enterprise limits is designed to provide transparency and accountability, ensuring that all stakeholders have
access to accurate and timely information on the institution’s credit risk exposures. The HCRM is responsible for the production
and distribution of all limit-related reports, which are generated using synthetic data to demonstrate the institution’s reporting
capabilities.
The primary reports include: (1) Daily Limit Utilization Report: Provides a snapshot of current exposure utilization against
approved limits, with alerts for any breaches or potential breaches. This report is distributed to the CPC, Business Line Heads,
and the HCRM; (2) Monthly Limit Monitoring Report: Includes a detailed analysis of limit utilization trends, breaches,
exceptions, and emerging risks. This report is presented to the CPC and the Board Risk Committee; and (3) Quarterly
Enterprise Risk Report: Provides a comprehensive view of the institution’s credit risk profile, including limit utilization,
concentrations, stress test results, and recommendations for adjustments to the limit framework.
All reports are subject to quality assurance checks by the HCRM to ensure accuracy and completeness. The reports include
clear visualizations, such as charts and graphs, to facilitate the interpretation of data and the identification of trends or outliers.
Synthetic data is used to populate these visualizations, ensuring that they are realistic and representative of the institution’s
actual reporting capabilities.
The CPC reviews the reports at each meeting and uses them to inform its decision-making on limit adjustments, exceptions,
and other policy changes. The Board Risk Committee receives the Quarterly Enterprise Risk Report and uses it to oversee the
institution’s credit risk management practices and ensure alignment with the risk appetite framework. Any material issues or
concerns identified in the reports are escalated to the Board for further action.
In addition to the standard reports, ad-hoc reporting may be requested by the CPC, the Board Risk Committee, or other
stakeholders to address specific concerns or emerging risks. The HCRM is responsible for producing these reports on a timely
basis, with the scope and content tailored to the needs of the requestor. All ad-hoc reports are documented and retained in
accordance with the institution’s record-keeping policy."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

