<urn:cpi:source:57:420> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 420" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:57> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "19"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "0171f0f0bb413e95a3c9a8253d8c5f71b9496025d505b0e93709b7366b224fc5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting and Disclosure
The bank’s RWA treatment processes are supported by a comprehensive reporting and disclosure framework, designed to
ensure transparency and accountability. The policy mandates that the Credit Risk Department (CRD) produce a monthly RWA
report, which is distributed to the Group Chief Risk Officer (CRO), the Risk Management Committee (RMC), and the Board of
Directors. The report includes a detailed breakdown of the bank’s RWA by business unit, sector, geography, and risk
classification, as well as a comparison against internal limits and regulatory requirements.
In addition to the monthly report, the policy requires that the CRD produce a quarterly RWA dashboard, which provides a
high-level overview of the bank’s RWA trends and concentrations. The dashboard includes key metrics such as the CET1
capital ratio, RWA density (RWA as a percentage of total assets), and the distribution of RWA across different risk buckets. The
dashboard is designed to facilitate strategic decision-making by the bank’s senior management and the Board.
The policy also mandates that the bank disclose its RWA treatment methodologies and key metrics in its annual financial
statements and Pillar 3 regulatory reports. The disclosures include a description of the bank’s RWA calculation approaches, the
use of internal models (where applicable), and the impact of RWA on the bank’s capital adequacy. The disclosures are
reviewed by the bank’s Legal and Compliance functions to ensure compliance with regulatory requirements and industry best
practices.
Internal reporting is supplemented by ad-hoc reports, which are produced in response to specific requests from senior
management, the RMC, or regulatory authorities. These reports may focus on specific aspects of the bank’s RWA treatment
processes, such as the performance of internal models, the effectiveness of risk mitigation techniques, or the impact of recent
regulatory changes. Ad-hoc reports are typically produced within five business days of the request.
This synthetic reporting and disclosure section demonstrates how a bank might ensure transparency in its RWA treatment
processes. It includes fictional reporting frequencies, roles, and metrics to illustrate the importance of regular and
comprehensive reporting in maintaining compliance with Basel IV and internal governance standards. All reporting
requirements described herein are intended for demonstration purposes only."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "Basel IV"^^xsd:string .

