    cpi:synthetic true .

<urn:cpi:source:15:112> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 112" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Review
This clause, '3.1 DSCR Minimums,' is subject to an annual review to ensure it remains aligned with the bank's risk appetite,
regulatory expectations, and industry best practices. The review is conducted by the Credit Policy Owner in collaboration with
the RCRC, the Underwriting Standards Team, and other relevant stakeholders. The review process includes an assessment of
the policy's effectiveness, the appropriateness of the DSCR thresholds, and the adequacy of the controls and processes in
place.
The annual review begins with a comprehensive analysis of the bank's retail credit portfolio, including trends in DSCR
compliance, the performance of loans approved with exceptions, and any emerging risks or issues. This analysis is supported
by data from the bank's loan origination system, credit monitoring tools, and internal audit reports. The Credit Policy Owner also
considers feedback from underwriters, credit managers, and other stakeholders who are involved in the day-to-day application
of the policy.
Based on the findings of the analysis, the Credit Policy Owner develops recommendations for updates or adjustments to the
clause. These recommendations may include changes to the DSCR thresholds, enhancements to the underwriting criteria, or
improvements to the monitoring and reporting processes. The recommendations are presented to the RCRC for review and
approval.
Once approved by the RCRC, any changes to the clause are documented and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The
Credit Policy Owner is responsible for ensuring that updates are implemented in a timely manner and that all employees are
trained on the revised requirements. The updated clause is then published in the bank's policy library, with a clear version
history to track changes over time.
In addition to the annual review, the clause may be updated more frequently in response to significant changes in the economic
environment, regulatory requirements, or the bank's risk appetite. The Credit Policy Owner is responsible for monitoring these
external factors and recommending interim updates as necessary. All changes to the clause, regardless of timing, must follow
the same approval and communication process to ensure consistency and transparency."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:21:135> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 135" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting
Reporting is a key component of the bank’s LTV Ceilings policy, providing transparency and accountability for all stakeholders.
The Retail Credit Risk Team is responsible for generating and distributing reports on LTV compliance, exceptions, and portfolio
performance to the RCRC, senior management, and regulatory bodies as required.
Monthly reports are generated to track compliance with LTV ceilings across all retail lending products. These reports include
metrics such as the number and percentage of loans approved within the standard LTV limits, the number and percentage of
exceptions, and the average LTV ratio for each product type. The reports also highlight any trends or anomalies, such as a
sudden increase in exceptions or a shift in LTV ratios for specific products.
Quarterly reports provide a more detailed analysis of LTV performance, including the impact of LTV ratios on loan defaults,
prepayments, and profitability. These reports are presented to the RCRC and include recommendations for policy adjustments,
if necessary. The RCRC uses these reports to assess the effectiveness of the LTV ceilings and to make data-driven decisions
about potential changes.
Annual reports are prepared for regulatory bodies and external auditors, summarizing the bank’s adherence to LTV ceilings and
any exceptions granted during the year. These reports include a detailed explanation of the bank’s LTV policy, the governance
framework, and the controls in place to ensure compliance. The reports are reviewed by the bank’s Legal and Compliance
teams before submission to regulators.
In addition to scheduled reports, ad-hoc reports may be generated in response to specific requests from senior management,
the RCRC, or regulatory bodies. These reports are tailored to address the specific concerns or questions raised and are
produced within a timeframe agreed upon with the requestor. All reports are stored in the bank’s secure document management
system and are accessible to authorized personnel for review and audit purposes."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:21:137> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 137" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Review
The LTV Ceilings clause is subject to periodic review to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with the
bank’s risk appetite and regulatory requirements. The review process is overseen by the RCRC, which is responsible for
evaluating the policy and recommending updates as needed.
The policy is reviewed annually, or more frequently if triggered by significant changes in market conditions, regulatory
requirements, or the bank’s risk profile. The annual review includes an assessment of the LTV ceilings’ performance in
mitigating risk, the adequacy of the quantitative limits, and the effectiveness of the governance, monitoring, and reporting
frameworks.
During the review process, the RCRC considers input from various stakeholders, including Retail Banking, Risk Management,
Compliance, and Legal. This input is gathered through surveys, interviews, or focus groups and is used to identify areas for
improvement or clarification. The RCRC also reviews industry best practices and benchmarking data to ensure the bank’s LTV
policy remains competitive and prudent.
If the review identifies the need for updates to the LTV ceilings or the policy framework, the RCRC will draft the proposed
changes and present them to the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) for approval. Once approved, the changes are communicated to all
relevant stakeholders through the bank’s policy management system, and training is provided to ensure that all employees are
aware of the updates.
The review process also includes a retrospective analysis of any exceptions granted under the policy. This analysis evaluates
whether the exceptions were justified, whether the compensating factors mitigated the additional risk, and whether the
outcomes (e.g., loan performance) supported the decision to grant the exception. The findings of this analysis are used to refine
the exception criteria and improve the overall policy framework.
Finally, the review process ensures that the LTV Ceilings clause remains internally consistent with the parent policy,
CP-Retail-v3.1, and with other related policies, such as the bank’s Collateral Valuation Policy and Underwriting Standards. Any
inconsistencies identified during the review are addressed promptly to maintain the integrity of the bank’s credit policy
framework."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:282> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 282" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1. Purpose and Objectives
This synthetic demonstration document, titled '1.1 RAROC Floors,' establishes the Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital (RAROC)
floors for credit transactions under the parent policy PRC-v1.7. The purpose of this policy is to ensure that all credit exposures
generate returns commensurate with their risk, aligning with the bank’s strategic objectives of sustainable profitability and
risk-adjusted performance optimization. By setting explicit RAROC floors, the bank aims to prevent the erosion of economic
value through suboptimal risk-taking and to foster a culture of disciplined credit origination and portfolio management.
The primary objective of this policy is to define minimum acceptable RAROC thresholds for various credit asset classes,
ensuring that each transaction or portfolio segment contributes positively to the bank’s economic profit. These thresholds are
designed to account for the cost of capital, expected losses, and operational expenses, thereby reflecting the true economic
value of credit activities. The policy also seeks to harmonize risk appetite across business units by providing a consistent
framework for evaluating creditworthiness and pricing risk.
This document is synthetic and intended for demonstration purposes only. It does not reflect the actual policies, thresholds, or
practices of any real financial institution. All numerical values, roles, and processes described herein are fictional and designed
to illustrate a comprehensive credit policy framework.
The RAROC floors established in this policy serve as a critical control mechanism to mitigate the risk of adverse selection and
to ensure that credit extensions are only made when they meet or exceed the bank’s hurdle rates. This aligns with the broader
goal of maintaining a robust and resilient credit portfolio that can withstand economic downturns while delivering consistent
shareholder value. The policy is integral to the bank’s enterprise-wide risk management framework and supports compliance
with internal governance standards and external regulatory expectations.
Additionally, this policy aims to promote transparency and accountability in credit decision-making by requiring explicit
justification for any deviations from the established RAROC floors. Such deviations must be supported by rigorous analysis and
approved through defined escalation paths, ensuring that exceptions are rare, well-documented, and aligned with the bank’s
strategic priorities."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:286> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 286" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance and Roles
The governance framework for this policy is overseen by the Credit Policy Committee (CPC), which is responsible for its
approval, periodic review, and amendment. The CPC is chaired by the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) and includes representatives
from Credit Risk, Finance, Business Units, and Independent Risk Control. The committee meets quarterly to assess the
effectiveness of the policy and to approve any proposed changes to RAROC floors or related thresholds.
The implementation of this policy is the responsibility of the Credit Risk Management (CRM) department, which is tasked with
ensuring that all credit exposures adhere to the established RAROC floors. CRM is also responsible for developing
methodologies, tools, and training programs to support the consistent application of the policy across the bank. The Head of
CRM reports directly to the CRO and provides regular updates on policy compliance and exceptions.
Business Unit Heads are accountable for ensuring that their respective teams adhere to the policy and that all credit proposals
submitted for approval meet the minimum RAROC requirements. They are also responsible for escalating any potential
breaches or exceptions to the CRM department for review and approval. Relationship managers and credit analysts are
required to incorporate RAROC calculations into their credit assessments and to document any deviations from the policy.
The Finance department plays a critical role in providing the cost of capital and other financial inputs required for RAROC
calculations. It is also responsible for validating the economic profit models used to determine RAROC and for ensuring that the
policy’s thresholds are aligned with the bank’s overall financial objectives. The Internal Audit function conducts periodic reviews
of the policy’s implementation to verify compliance and to identify any gaps or areas for improvement.
For this synthetic policy, the governance structure is designed to reflect industry best practices, with clear segregation of duties
and escalation paths. All roles and responsibilities are documented in the bank’s broader Credit Policy Framework, which this
document supplements."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:299> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 299" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting and Transparency
The bank maintains a comprehensive reporting framework to ensure transparency and accountability in the application of
RAROC floors. Monthly RAROC compliance reports are generated by the CRM department and distributed to Business Unit
Heads, the CPC, and the CRO. These reports include a summary of all exposures by asset class, risk rating, and RAROC
performance, as well as a detailed breakdown of exceptions and their status.
The monthly reports also highlight trends in RAROC performance, such as deteriorating or improving segments, and provide an
analysis of the drivers behind these trends. This includes changes in market conditions, credit spreads, or the bank’s cost of
capital. The reports are designed to facilitate proactive risk management and to inform strategic decisions regarding portfolio
composition and risk appetite.
Quarterly, the CRM department prepares a more detailed RAROC Performance Review, which is presented to the CPC and the
Board Risk Committee. This review includes an assessment of the bank’s overall RAROC performance, a comparison against
peer benchmarks, and an evaluation of the effectiveness of the RAROC floors in achieving the bank’s risk-adjusted return
objectives. The review also identifies any emerging risks or opportunities for improvement.
In addition to internal reporting, the bank includes RAROC metrics in its external disclosures, such as the annual report and
pillar 3 regulatory filings. These disclosures provide stakeholders with insight into the bank’s risk-adjusted performance and its
adherence to internal policies. The bank is committed to maintaining the confidentiality of client-specific information while
ensuring transparency in its aggregate RAROC performance.
Ad-hoc reporting is also available upon request from senior management, regulators, or other stakeholders. The CRM
department is responsible for ensuring that all reports are accurate, timely, and aligned with the bank’s data governance
standards. Any discrepancies or errors in reporting must be investigated and corrected promptly, with root cause analysis
conducted to prevent recurrence."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:301> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 301" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Amendments
This policy is subject to annual review by the CPC to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with the
bank’s strategic objectives and risk appetite. The review process includes an assessment of the policy’s performance against its
stated objectives, an evaluation of emerging risks and market developments, and a benchmarking exercise against industry
best practices.
Amendments to the policy, including adjustments to RAROC floors or changes to governance structures, must be approved by
the CPC and, where material, by the Board Risk Committee. Proposed amendments are typically developed by the CRM
department in consultation with relevant stakeholders, including Business Units, Finance, and Risk Control. The CRM
department is responsible for conducting impact assessments to evaluate the potential effects of proposed changes on the
bank’s credit portfolio and economic profit.
The policy review process also includes a retrospective analysis of exceptions and waivers granted under the policy, with a
focus on identifying any patterns or systemic issues. This analysis informs recommendations for policy enhancements, such as
the introduction of new asset class-specific floors, adjustments to escalation paths, or revisions to the RAROC calculation
methodology.
In addition to the annual review, the CPC may initiate an interim review of the policy in response to significant changes in the
bank’s risk profile, cost of capital, or external regulatory environment. Such reviews are conducted on an expedited basis, with
amendments implemented as soon as practicable. All changes to the policy are communicated to relevant stakeholders through
formal policy updates, training sessions, and internal memoranda.
This synthetic policy is designed to be a living document, evolving in response to the dynamic nature of the banking industry
and the bank’s strategic priorities. The CPC is committed to ensuring that the policy remains a robust and effective tool for
managing risk-adjusted returns, while also fostering a culture of continuous improvement and innovation in credit risk
management."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:52:317> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 317" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:52> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "a31614bc9583cac5c205cf3ac248b30964fb2ecb3c011a9d90d81ca1e5d5d963"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Exceptions and Escalation Paths
Exceptions to the Spread Grid are permitted only in limited circumstances and must be formally documented, justified, and
approved. An exception is defined as any transaction where the total spread falls outside the permitted range (100–700 bps) or
where adjustments exceed the caps specified in Section 5. Exceptions may arise due to unique transaction structures, strategic
considerations, or market dislocations that temporarily distort risk-based pricing.
The escalation path for exceptions depends on the magnitude of the deviation and the risk profile of the transaction. For
spreads between 500–700 bps or adjustments exceeding ±150 bps, the request must be escalated to the GCC for approval.
For spreads below 100 bps or above 700 bps, or adjustments exceeding ±200 bps, the request must be escalated to the CPC.
The escalation must include a detailed justification, supported by evidence such as market comparables, third-party valuations,
or stress-testing results.
The justification for an exception must address the following: (1) the specific circumstances warranting the deviation; (2) the
impact on the bank’s risk profile and capital requirements; (3) the mitigating factors (e.g., collateral, guarantees, or covenants);
and (4) the expected financial return relative to the risk. The request must also include a comparison to the Spread Grid’s base
spread and an explanation of why the standard adjustments are insufficient.
Once an exception is approved, it must be documented in the credit approval memorandum and entered into the bank’s
exception tracking system. The system monitors the performance of excepted exposures and flags any deterioration in credit
quality or compliance with covenants. Exceptions are subject to enhanced monitoring, including quarterly reviews by the CRM
department and annual validation by Internal Audit.
The CPC reviews all active exceptions at least semi-annually to assess whether the justifications remain valid and whether the
exposures continue to perform as expected. Exceptions that no longer meet the approval criteria must be repriced or unwound,
unless a new exception request is submitted and approved. The CPC may also impose additional controls, such as higher
capital charges or more frequent reporting, on excepted exposures."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:52:319> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 319" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:52> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "a31614bc9583cac5c205cf3ac248b30964fb2ecb3c011a9d90d81ca1e5d5d963"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting and Transparency
The bank maintains a comprehensive reporting framework to ensure transparency and accountability in the application of the
Spread Grid. The CRM department generates a monthly Spread Grid Compliance Report, which is distributed to the CPC,
business unit heads, and Internal Audit. The report includes metrics such as the number of transactions priced within the grid,
the frequency and magnitude of adjustments, the number of exceptions, and the average spread by risk bucket and business
unit.
The monthly report also highlights any control failures, such as transactions lacking required approvals or documentation, and
tracks the resolution of these issues. Business units are required to provide explanations for any material deviations from the
grid and to outline corrective actions. The CPC reviews the report to identify systemic issues or trends that may require policy
updates or additional training.
Quarterly, the CRM department produces a Spread Grid Performance Report, which analyzes the financial impact of the grid on
the bank’s portfolio. This report includes an assessment of the spreads’ effectiveness in compensating for credit losses, the
contribution of spreads to net interest margins, and the sensitivity of the portfolio to changes in market conditions. The report is
presented to the Board Risk Committee and used to inform strategic decisions, such as risk appetite adjustments or capital
allocation.
Annually, the bank publishes a summary of its Spread Grid framework in its Pillars 1 and 3 disclosures under Basel III. The
disclosure includes the methodology for determining spreads, the range of spreads applied to different risk buckets, and the
governance processes for approvals and exceptions. This transparency is intended to provide regulators, investors, and other
stakeholders with confidence in the bank’s risk management practices.
Ad hoc reporting is triggered by significant events, such as a breach of the bank’s risk appetite statement, a material change in
market conditions, or a regulatory inquiry. In such cases, the CRM department works with the affected business units to
produce a detailed analysis of the event’s impact on the Spread Grid and the broader credit portfolio. The findings are reported
to the CPC and, where necessary, to the Board of Directors."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:56:396> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 396" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:56> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "18"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "fd93de0b531f7b06b26a6a55c8dd136cbac2a641dd3fc8ac1611fa9ccfc04995"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """7. 7. Monitoring and Trigger Events
Monitoring is a critical component of the bank’s credit escalation framework, ensuring that risks are identified and addressed
proactively. The bank employs a multi-layered monitoring approach, combining automated surveillance tools with manual
reviews by credit and risk professionals. The primary objective is to detect early warning signals of credit deterioration, policy
breaches, or emerging risks that may require escalation under this policy.
The bank’s Credit Management System (CMS) includes a real-time monitoring module that tracks key credit metrics for all
counterparties, including financial ratios, risk ratings, covenant compliance, and exposure levels. The system generates
automated alerts when predefined thresholds are breached, such as a decline in a borrower’s risk rating, a breach of financial
covenants, or an increase in exposure beyond approved limits. These alerts are routed to the relevant Relationship Manager
and their supervisor, with escalation to higher authorities if the issue is not resolved within the stipulated timeframe.
In addition to automated monitoring, the bank conducts periodic manual reviews of credit portfolios to identify qualitative risks
that may not be captured by quantitative thresholds. For example, the Portfolio Risk Management team performs quarterly
reviews of high-risk industries, such as commercial real estate or leveraged lending, to assess the impact of macroeconomic
trends on credit quality. These reviews may trigger escalations to the RCO or CCO if they identify systemic risks or the need for
portfolio-level actions, such as increased provisions or reduced exposures.
Trigger events that mandate escalation under this policy include, but are not limited to, the following: a borrower’s risk rating
downgrade by two or more notches within a 12-month period; a breach of any financial or non-financial covenant that is not
cured within the grace period; a material adverse change in the borrower’s business, such as a significant loss of market share,
regulatory action, or litigation; or the identification of fraud, misrepresentation, or other integrity issues. The RM must escalate
such events to the BUCO within 24 hours of identification, with further escalation to the RCO or CCO as determined by the
severity of the issue.
The bank’s Internal Audit function conducts independent reviews of the monitoring and escalation processes at least annually.
These reviews assess the effectiveness of automated alerts, the timeliness of escalations, and the adequacy of actions taken to
address identified risks. Findings from these reviews are reported to the CCO and the Board’s Audit Committee, with corrective
actions tracked to completion. The CCO may also commission targeted reviews of specific portfolios or business units in
response to emerging risks or regulatory concerns."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:58:424> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 424" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:58> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "20"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "124566926bfd9f4ebe2d18795df04f73f70fba22a895c4181d767a9bf0156aaa"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1. Purpose
The purpose of this Capital Floors Policy (the 'Policy') is to establish a robust framework for the implementation, monitoring, and
enforcement of capital floor requirements in accordance with the Basel IV regulatory standards. This Policy ensures that the
Bank maintains a minimum level of capital that mitigates risks arising from underestimation or miscalculation of risk-weighted
assets (RWAs) under internal models. By setting a floor, the Bank guarantees that its capital adequacy ratio does not fall below
a specified threshold, thereby safeguarding financial stability and regulatory compliance.
This Policy is designed to address the potential discrepancies between the Bank’s internal risk assessments and the
standardized approaches prescribed by regulatory authorities. It ensures that the Bank’s capital planning and risk management
practices are aligned with the principles of prudence, transparency, and accountability. The Policy also serves as a critical
control mechanism to prevent excessive leverage and to maintain public trust in the Bank’s financial resilience.
The Capital Floors Policy is a synthetic demonstration document created for illustrative purposes. It reflects industry best
practices and regulatory expectations but does not represent the actual policies, procedures, or practices of any real financial
institution. All thresholds, roles, and processes described herein are fictional and intended solely for training, testing, or
educational use.
This Policy applies universally across all business units, subsidiaries, and jurisdictions in which the Bank operates. It is binding
on all employees, contractors, and third parties acting on behalf of the Bank. Compliance with this Policy is mandatory, and any
deviations must be justified, documented, and approved through the established governance framework.
The Policy is reviewed annually or more frequently if there are significant changes in regulatory requirements, the Bank’s risk
profile, or its operational environment. The review process ensures that the Policy remains relevant, effective, and aligned with
the Bank’s strategic objectives and regulatory obligations."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "Basel IV"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:58:430> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 430" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:58> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "20"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "124566926bfd9f4ebe2d18795df04f73f70fba22a895c4181d767a9bf0156aaa"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria outlined in this section are designed to ensure that all credit exposures subject to the Capital Floors
Policy are originated and managed in a manner consistent with the Bank’s risk appetite and regulatory requirements. These
criteria apply to all new and renewed exposures, including loans, derivatives, and other credit facilities.
For credit exposures assessed under the IRB approach, the Bank must ensure that the estimated Probability of Default (PD),
Loss Given Default (LGD), and Exposure at Default (EAD) parameters are conservative and supported by robust historical data
and forward-looking analysis. The PD, LGD, and EAD estimates must be validated annually by an independent party, such as
the Model Validation Team (MVT), to ensure their accuracy and reliability.
The Bank’s underwriting process must incorporate a capital floor assessment for all material exposures. This assessment
involves comparing the RWAs calculated under the IRB approach with those calculated under the standardized approach. If the
IRB RWAs are less than 72.5% of the standardized RWAs, the difference must be justified and documented. Justifications must
be based on objective evidence, such as the quality of the underlying data, the robustness of the model, or the specific risk
characteristics of the exposure.
For exposures where the capital floor is breached, the underwriting process must include an escalation to the RMC for
approval. The escalation must include a detailed analysis of the reasons for the breach, the potential risks, and the proposed
mitigation measures. The RMC may approve the exposure if it is satisfied that the risks are adequately managed and that the
breach does not pose a material threat to the Bank’s capital adequacy.
The underwriting criteria also require that all credit exposures be subject to ongoing monitoring and review. This includes
regular updates to the PD, LGD, and EAD parameters, as well as periodic reassessments of the capital floor compliance. Any
material changes in the risk profile of an exposure must be reported to the RMD and, if necessary, escalated to the RMC."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "Basel IV"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:59:480> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
