<urn:cpi:source:13:67> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 67" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Exceptions
Exceptions to the tenor limits outlined in Clause 4.3 are permitted under specific circumstances, provided that they are justified
by the borrower’s creditworthiness, the purpose of the facility, and the bank’s strategic objectives. All exceptions must be
documented, approved by the appropriate authority, and subject to enhanced monitoring and reporting.
The most common justification for an exception is the presence of strong mitigating factors, such as high-quality collateral,
third-party guarantees, or the borrower’s demonstrated ability to generate stable and predictable cash flows over the extended
tenor. Exceptions may also be granted for facilities that support strategic transactions, such as large-scale infrastructure
projects or long-term trade finance deals, where the bank has a strong commercial interest.
The process for requesting an exception begins with the submission of a credit proposal by the business unit, which includes a
detailed rationale for the exception, an analysis of the borrower’s financial strength, and an assessment of the impact on the
bank’s risk profile. The proposal must also include a plan for monitoring the facility and mitigating any additional risks introduced
by the extended tenor.
Once submitted, the proposal is reviewed by CRM, which assesses the justification for the exception and the adequacy of the
risk mitigants. CRM may request additional information or analysis from the business unit before making a recommendation.
For Tier 1 and Tier 2 exceptions, CRM’s recommendation is forwarded to the TLO or the Business Unit Head and CRO,
respectively, for approval. For Tier 3 exceptions, the recommendation is forwarded to the GCC.
All approved exceptions are documented in the bank’s exception register, which is maintained by CRM and reviewed by the
GCC on a quarterly basis. The register includes details of the exception, the rationale for approval, the mitigating factors, and
the enhanced monitoring and reporting requirements. Exceptions are also subject to post-approval audits by Internal Audit to
ensure compliance with the approved terms and conditions."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:13:71> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 71" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Review
Clause 4.3 Tenor Limits is subject to regular review to ensure that it remains aligned with the bank’s risk appetite, market
conditions, and regulatory requirements. The review process is designed to assess the policy’s effectiveness, identify any gaps
or deficiencies, and recommend adjustments as necessary to enhance the bank’s credit risk management framework.
The policy is reviewed annually by CRM, in consultation with the business units, Compliance, and Internal Audit. The annual
review includes an assessment of the policy’s adherence to regulatory requirements, its alignment with industry best practices,
and its effectiveness in managing tenor-related risks. The review also considers feedback from business units and other
stakeholders on the policy’s practicality and any challenges encountered in its implementation.
In addition to the annual review, the policy is subject to ad hoc reviews in response to significant changes in the bank’s risk
appetite, market conditions, or regulatory environment. For example, a material deterioration in economic conditions or a
change in the bank’s strategic priorities may prompt a review of the tenor limits to ensure that they remain appropriate and
effective.
The review process includes a comprehensive analysis of the bank’s tenor exposure and the performance of facilities with
non-standard tenors. This analysis assesses whether the existing limits and exceptions have contributed to the bank’s risk
profile in a manner consistent with its risk appetite and whether any adjustments are needed to better align the policy with the
bank’s objectives.
The results of the review are documented in a Tenor Limit Policy Review Report, which is presented to the GCC for approval.
The report includes a summary of the review’s findings, any recommended changes to the policy, and a plan for implementing
those changes. Once approved by the GCC, the updated policy is communicated to all relevant stakeholders, and training is
provided as necessary to ensure that the changes are effectively implemented."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:15:108> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 108" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Exceptions
Exceptions to the DSCR minimums outlined in this clause are permitted only under specific circumstances and with appropriate
approvals. An exception may be considered for a borrower who falls below the minimum DSCR threshold but demonstrates
strong compensating factors, such as a high credit score, stable and predictable income, or significant liquid assets. However,
no exceptions are permitted for borrowers with a DSCR below the hard floor of 1.00x.
The exception process begins with the underwriter or relationship manager, who must document the borrower's compensating
factors and provide a rationale for why the exception is warranted. This documentation must include a detailed analysis of the
borrower's financial position, the specific risks involved, and the mitigating factors that justify the exception. The request is then
escalated to a credit manager for review and approval.
Credit managers have the authority to approve exceptions for loans up to $250,000. For loans exceeding this amount, the
exception must be escalated to the Head of Retail Credit or their designated delegate. Exceptions for loans over $1,000,000
require approval from the RCRC. All exceptions, regardless of size, must be documented in the bank's exception tracking
system and reported to the RCRC on a monthly basis.
Borrowers who are granted an exception to the DSCR minimums are subject to enhanced monitoring. This includes more
frequent financial reporting, such as quarterly income and expense statements, and regular contact with the relationship
manager to discuss their financial position. The bank may also impose additional covenants or conditions on the loan, such as
maintaining a minimum liquidity balance or restricting additional borrowing.
The RCRC conducts a quarterly review of all DSCR exceptions to ensure they remain appropriate and that the compensating
factors continue to mitigate the risk. If a borrower's financial position deteriorates further or if the compensating factors are no
longer present, the bank may take corrective action, such as requiring the borrower to pay down the loan balance or converting
the loan to a more restrictive product. Exceptions that are no longer justified must be reported to the Internal Audit Department
for further investigation."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:48:236> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 236" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Exceptions and Waivers
Exceptions to the coverage ratio policy may be granted in limited circumstances where the bank determines that the overall
credit risk is acceptable despite the deviation from standard thresholds. Exceptions must be justified by compelling mitigating
factors, such as strong sponsorship, additional guarantees, or structural subordination of other creditors. All exceptions must be
documented in the Credit Policy Exception Log and approved through the escalation paths outlined in Section 6.
Temporary waivers may be granted for coverage ratio breaches that are expected to be short-term in nature. For example, a
borrower may experience a temporary decline in cash flow due to a one-time expense, such as a major capital expenditure. In
such cases, the bank may grant a temporary waiver of the DSCR covenant, provided the borrower demonstrates a clear path to
restoring compliance within a specified timeframe.
Permanent waivers may be granted for coverage ratio breaches that are expected to persist over the long term. For example, a
borrower in a cyclical industry may experience prolonged periods of low cash flow due to market conditions. In such cases, the
bank may grant a permanent waiver of the DSCR covenant, provided the borrower maintains other financial metrics, such as a
strong balance sheet or liquidity position.
The policy requires that all exceptions and waivers be reviewed at least annually to ensure that the mitigating factors remain
valid and that the overall credit risk remains acceptable. The Credit Monitoring team is responsible for tracking the performance
of exceptions and waivers and reporting any issues to the CRC. Exceptions that are no longer justified must be resolved
through corrective actions, such as loan restructuring, additional collateral, or repayment.
This synthetic exceptions framework illustrates the bank’s approach to managing deviations from policy in a controlled and
transparent manner. The exceptions and waivers outlined herein are designed to demonstrate the bank’s flexibility in
accommodating unique borrower circumstances while maintaining prudent risk management. Actual exception practices may
vary based on the bank’s risk appetite, regulatory requirements, and market conditions."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:50:266> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 266" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:50> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance
The governance of this Haircuts Clause is overseen by the bank’s Credit Risk Committee (CRC), which is responsible for
approving, monitoring, and updating the haircut parameters. The CRC is chaired by the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) and includes
representatives from Credit, Risk Management, Legal, Compliance, and Finance. The committee meets quarterly to review the
effectiveness of the haircut framework and to approve any adjustments based on market conditions or regulatory changes.
Day-to-day governance is delegated to the Collateral Management Unit (CMU), which reports to the CRC. The CMU is
responsible for implementing the haircut policy, ensuring compliance, and providing training to relevant staff. The CMU also
conducts periodic audits to verify that haircuts are applied correctly and consistently across all business lines.
The governance structure includes a clear escalation path for issues or disputes related to haircut applications. Any concerns
regarding the appropriateness of a haircut percentage or its application must be escalated to the CMU, which will investigate
and resolve the matter in consultation with the relevant business unit. If the issue remains unresolved, it will be escalated to the
CRC for final determination.
The bank’s Internal Audit function plays a critical role in governance by conducting independent reviews of the haircut policy’s
implementation. Audit findings are reported to the CRC and the bank’s Board of Directors, ensuring that any deficiencies are
addressed promptly. The Internal Audit team also validates that the haircut parameters are aligned with the bank’s risk appetite
and regulatory requirements.
Governance also includes the documentation and maintenance of a haircut parameter database, which is managed by the
CMU. This database contains the current haircut percentages for all eligible collateral types, along with the rationale and
supporting data for each. The database is updated at least annually or more frequently if market conditions warrant. This clause
is synthetic demonstration data and does not reflect actual governance structures."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:50:268> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 268" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:50> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria for applying haircuts under this clause are designed to ensure that collateral values are conservatively
estimated to account for potential risks. The primary criterion is the liquidity and volatility of the collateral asset. Highly liquid
assets, such as cash or government securities, are subject to lower haircuts, while less liquid or more volatile assets, such as
private equity or distressed debt, receive higher haircuts.
Collateral eligibility is determined by the bank’s Collateral Acceptance Policy, which defines the types of assets that can be
pledged. Only assets that meet the bank’s eligibility criteria are subject to the haircuts outlined in this clause. Ineligible assets
must not be accepted as collateral under any circumstances, regardless of the proposed haircut.
The underwriting process includes a thorough assessment of the collateral’s market value, which is determined using
recognized valuation methodologies. For marketable securities, this typically involves using the most recent market prices or
third-party valuations. For non-marketable assets, such as real estate or inventory, independent appraisals or internal models
are used. The valuation must be documented and retained for audit purposes.
In addition to liquidity and volatility, the underwriting criteria consider the correlation between the collateral and the borrower’s
financial health. For example, if the collateral is closely tied to the borrower’s business (e.g., inventory for a manufacturing
company), the haircut may be increased to account for the potential simultaneous deterioration of the borrower’s
creditworthiness and the collateral’s value.
The underwriting criteria also include a review of the legal and operational risks associated with the collateral. This includes
verifying the bank’s perfected security interest in the collateral and assessing any restrictions on the bank’s ability to liquidate
the asset in the event of default. Any legal or operational concerns must be resolved before the collateral is accepted. This
section is part of a synthetic demonstration dataset."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:50:274> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 274" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:50> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """7. 7. Monitoring
The bank employs a robust monitoring framework to ensure that haircuts remain appropriate and effective throughout the life of
the loan. The Collateral Management Unit (CMU) is responsible for ongoing monitoring of collateral values and haircut
applications, with support from the Risk Management and Operations teams.
Monitoring includes daily price verification for marketable securities and periodic revaluation for non-marketable assets. For
marketable securities, the CMU uses automated systems to track market prices and apply haircuts in real-time. If the market
value of the collateral falls below the required threshold (e.g., 80% of the loan value for a 20% haircut), the system triggers an
alert for immediate action, such as requesting additional collateral or liquidating the asset.
Non-marketable assets, such as real estate or inventory, are revalued at least annually or more frequently if there are
indications of material changes in value. The revaluation process involves independent appraisals or internal assessments, and
the results are compared against the original valuation to determine if the haircut remains adequate. Any significant decline in
value may trigger a margin call or a requirement for additional collateral.
The monitoring framework also includes regular stress testing of the haircut parameters. The CMU conducts quarterly stress
tests to assess the potential impact of adverse market conditions on collateral values. The results of these tests are reported to
the Credit Risk Committee (CRC) and used to adjust haircut percentages if necessary.
In addition to automated monitoring, the CMU conducts periodic manual reviews of a sample of collateralized loans to verify
compliance with the haircut policy. These reviews include checking the accuracy of haircut applications, the timeliness of
revaluations, and the adequacy of documentation. Findings from these reviews are documented and addressed through
corrective actions. This clause is synthetic demonstration data."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:50:280> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 280" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:50> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Review
The Haircuts Clause (Clause 2.3) is subject to regular review to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment
with the bank’s risk management objectives. The review process is overseen by the Credit Risk Committee (CRC) and involves
input from the Collateral Management Unit (CMU), Risk Management, Legal, Compliance, and business units.
The policy is reviewed at least annually, or more frequently if there are significant changes in market conditions, regulatory
requirements, or the bank’s risk appetite. The annual review includes an assessment of the haircut parameters’ performance,
based on data from the past year, including the frequency of margin calls, the adequacy of collateral coverage, and the
outcomes of stress tests.
The review process begins with a comprehensive analysis conducted by the CMU, which evaluates the policy’s strengths and
weaknesses. The CMU presents its findings to the CRC, along with recommendations for updates or adjustments. The CRC
reviews the analysis and recommendations, considering factors such as the bank’s strategic goals, regulatory developments,
and industry best practices.
Stakeholder feedback is a critical component of the review process. The CMU solicits input from business units, risk managers,
and other relevant parties to identify practical challenges or areas for improvement in the haircut policy. This feedback is
incorporated into the CRC’s deliberations and may lead to revisions in the policy or its implementation guidelines.
Once the CRC approves any changes to the haircut policy, the updated clause is communicated to all relevant parties, and
training is provided as necessary. The revised policy is then implemented, and its effectiveness is monitored closely during the
subsequent review period. This section is part of a synthetic demonstration dataset and does not reflect actual review
processes."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:59:482> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 482" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:59> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
