<urn:cpi:source:21:123> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 123" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance
Governance of the LTV Ceilings clause is overseen by the Retail Credit Risk Committee (RCRC), which is responsible for
approving, monitoring, and updating the policy in alignment with the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory expectations. The
RCRC reports directly to the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) and meets quarterly to review the effectiveness and relevance of the LTV
ceilings.
The RCRC is composed of senior representatives from Retail Banking, Risk Management, Compliance, and Legal
departments. This cross-functional composition ensures that the LTV ceilings are evaluated from multiple perspectives,
including risk, customer impact, and regulatory compliance. Decisions made by the RCRC are documented in meeting minutes
and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.
Day-to-day governance is delegated to the Retail Credit Policy Team, which is responsible for implementing the LTV ceilings,
providing guidance to underwriters, and monitoring adherence to the policy. This team also conducts periodic audits to ensure
that LTV calculations are accurate and that exceptions are properly documented and approved.
The governance framework includes a clear escalation path for issues or disputes related to LTV ceilings. Underwriters or loan
officers who encounter situations where the LTV ceiling may need to be exceeded must escalate the case to their direct
supervisor, who will then consult with the Retail Credit Policy Team. If the issue remains unresolved, it is escalated to the
RCRC for a final decision.
To ensure transparency and accountability, all governance activities related to LTV ceilings, including policy updates, audits,
and exception approvals, are documented in the bank’s policy management system. This system is accessible to all authorized
personnel and is subject to internal and external audits."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:21:129> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 129" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approvals
The approval process for loans subject to LTV ceilings is designed to ensure compliance with the policy while maintaining
efficiency in the underwriting workflow. All loan applications must be reviewed by an authorized underwriter, who is responsible
for verifying that the LTV ratio does not exceed the applicable ceiling. The underwriter’s approval is required before a loan can
proceed to funding.
For loans that comply with the standard LTV ceilings, the underwriter has the authority to approve the application, provided all
other underwriting criteria (e.g., credit score, DTI ratio) are met. However, if the LTV ratio exceeds the standard ceiling, the
underwriter must escalate the case to their direct supervisor for further review. The supervisor may approve the exception if
justified by compensating factors, such as a strong borrower profile or additional collateral.
Loans that exceed the LTV ceiling by more than 5% require approval from the Retail Credit Policy Team. The team will evaluate
the request based on the borrower’s risk profile, the quality of the collateral, and the overall portfolio risk. If the exception is
approved, the Retail Credit Policy Team will document the rationale and any conditions (e.g., higher interest rate, additional
collateral) in the loan file.
Exceptions to the LTV ceiling that exceed 10% of the standard limit must be escalated to the RCRC for approval. The RCRC
will conduct a thorough review, including an assessment of the borrower’s financial stability, the collateral’s value, and the
potential impact on the bank’s risk exposure. Approval from the RCRC is final and must be documented in the policy
management system.
All approvals, including standard underwriting approvals and exceptions, must be recorded in the bank’s loan origination system
(LOS). The LOS will automatically flag any loans that exceed the LTV ceiling for review and will prevent funding until the
appropriate approvals are obtained. This control ensures that no loan is funded in violation of the LTV policy."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:48:234> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 234" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """7. 7. Monitoring and Covenants
Monitoring of coverage ratios is a critical component of the bank’s credit risk management framework. Borrowers are required
to provide financial statements and other relevant data on a quarterly basis to enable the bank to recalculate coverage ratios
and assess compliance with covenants. The bank’s Credit Monitoring team is responsible for tracking ratio performance and
identifying any trends or deviations that may indicate deteriorating credit quality.
Covenants related to coverage ratios are typically included in credit agreements to provide early warning of potential credit
issues. For example, a credit agreement may include a covenant requiring the borrower to maintain a DSCR of at least 1.25x. If
the borrower’s DSCR falls below this threshold, the covenant is breached, and the bank may take actions such as accelerating
the loan, increasing the interest rate, or requiring additional collateral.
The policy requires that covenants be tailored to the specific risks of the borrower and the transaction. For example, a borrower
with volatile cash flows may be subject to a rolling 12-month DSCR covenant, while a borrower with stable cash flows may be
subject to an annual DSCR covenant. Similarly, a borrower with a high proportion of variable-rate debt may be subject to an
ICR covenant to mitigate interest rate risk.
In addition to financial covenants, the policy requires that borrowers provide regular updates on the status of their collateral. For
example, borrowers pledging real estate as collateral may be required to provide annual appraisals, while borrowers pledging
inventory may be required to provide quarterly inventory reports. The bank’s Collateral Management team is responsible for
reviewing these updates and assessing the ongoing adequacy of the collateral coverage.
This section of the synthetic policy demonstrates the bank’s approach to proactive credit monitoring. The covenants and
monitoring requirements outlined herein are illustrative and designed to ensure early detection of credit issues. Actual
covenants and monitoring practices may vary based on the bank’s risk appetite, regulatory requirements, and the specific
characteristics of the borrower and transaction."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:288> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 288" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. RAROC Methodology and Calculation
The RAROC for each credit exposure is calculated using the bank’s internal economic profit model, which adjusts nominal
returns for risk, cost of capital, and operational expenses. The formula for RAROC is defined as: RAROC = (Risk-Adjusted
Revenue - Expected Loss - Operating Costs) / Economic Capital. Risk-Adjusted Revenue reflects the net interest income and
fees associated with the exposure, adjusted for credit spreads and other risk premiums.
Economic Capital is determined using the bank’s internal capital allocation model, which assigns capital to each exposure
based on its risk characteristics, including probability of default (PD), loss given default (LGD), and exposure at default (EAD).
The model incorporates both systematic and idiosyncratic risk factors and is calibrated to a 99.9% confidence interval over a
one-year horizon, in line with Basel III standards.
Expected Loss is calculated as the product of PD, LGD, and EAD, representing the average annual loss expected from the
exposure. Operating Costs include all direct and indirect expenses associated with originating, servicing, and monitoring the
credit exposure, such as underwriting fees, legal costs, and administrative overhead. These costs are allocated to individual
exposures based on their complexity and resource requirements.
The bank employs a tiered RAROC floor structure, with minimum thresholds varying by asset class, risk rating, and tenure. For
example, investment-grade corporate exposures are subject to a minimum RAROC floor of 12%, while sub-investment-grade
exposures require a floor of 18%. Commercial real estate exposures are assigned a floor of 15%, reflecting their higher volatility
and illiquidity risk. These thresholds are reviewed annually by the CPC and adjusted based on changes in the bank’s cost of
capital, risk appetite, or market conditions.
All RAROC calculations must be performed using the bank’s approved modeling tools and validated by the CRM department.
Manual overrides or adjustments to the model inputs are prohibited unless explicitly approved by the CRO. The methodology
and assumptions underlying the RAROC calculations are documented in the bank’s Model Risk Management Policy, which is
referenced in this document."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:56:402> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 402" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:56> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "18"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "fd93de0b531f7b06b26a6a55c8dd136cbac2a641dd3fc8ac1611fa9ccfc04995"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Continuous Improvement
This policy is subject to regular review to ensure that it remains aligned with the bank’s risk appetite, regulatory requirements,
and industry best practices. The review process is designed to identify opportunities for improvement, address emerging risks,
and incorporate lessons learned from the bank’s credit activities and external developments. The Chief Credit Officer (CCO) is
responsible for overseeing the review process, with input from the Credit Policy Committee (CPC), Risk Management Division
(RMD), and other relevant stakeholders.
The policy is reviewed at least annually, or more frequently if triggered by material changes in the bank’s operating
environment. Trigger events for an ad-hoc review include, but are not limited to, significant credit losses, regulatory changes,
mergers or acquisitions, or shifts in the bank’s strategic direction. The CCO may also initiate a review in response to findings
from internal audits, external examinations, or risk assessments that identify gaps or deficiencies in the escalation framework.
The review process begins with an assessment of the policy’s effectiveness, which is conducted by the CPC in collaboration
with the RMD. This assessment includes an analysis of escalation data from the Credit Management System (CMS), such as
the volume and value of escalated transactions, the reasons for escalation, and the outcomes of the escalation process. The
assessment also evaluates the timeliness of escalations, the adequacy of approval workflows, and the effectiveness of
monitoring and reporting mechanisms. Feedback is solicited from business units, risk professionals, and other stakeholders to
identify practical challenges or areas for improvement.
Based on the findings of the assessment, the CPC develops recommendations for updates to the policy. These
recommendations may include revisions to quantitative thresholds, underwriting criteria, or approval workflows, as well as
enhancements to the CMS or other supporting systems. The CPC presents its recommendations to the CCO, who reviews
them for alignment with the bank’s risk appetite and strategic objectives. If the CCO approves the recommendations, they are
formalized into a revised version of the policy and submitted to the Board’s Risk Committee for ratification.
Once approved, the revised policy is communicated to all relevant stakeholders through the bank’s policy management system,
with a clear implementation timeline and any required training or system updates. The CCO is responsible for ensuring that the
revised policy is effectively implemented and that all employees are aware of their obligations under the new framework. The
bank’s Internal Audit function may conduct a post-implementation review to verify compliance with the revised policy and to
assess its effectiveness in addressing the identified issues.
Continuous improvement is a core principle of the bank’s credit risk management framework. The CCO encourages a culture of
open communication and feedback, where employees at all levels are empowered to identify and report potential improvements
to the escalation process. The bank also benchmarks its policies and practices against industry peers and regulatory
expectations to ensure that it remains at the forefront of credit risk management. By fostering a proactive and adaptive
approach to policy review, the bank aims to maintain a robust and resilient credit escalation framework that supports its
long-term success."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:59:460> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
