<urn:cpi:source:13:69> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 69" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting
Reporting is a key control mechanism for ensuring transparency and accountability in the management of tenor limits. The
bank’s reporting framework for Clause 4.3 Tenor Limits is designed to provide timely and accurate information to senior
management, the GCC, and other stakeholders, enabling them to monitor compliance, assess risk exposures, and make
informed decisions.
CRM is responsible for preparing and distributing a monthly Tenor Limit Compliance Report, which provides a summary of all
new credit facilities approved during the month, including their tenors, borrower credit ratings, and any exceptions granted. The
report also highlights any breaches of the tenor limits and the actions taken to remediate them.
In addition to the monthly compliance report, CRM prepares a quarterly Tenor Risk Exposure Report, which provides a
comprehensive analysis of the bank’s aggregate tenor exposure. This report includes a breakdown of the portfolio by tenor,
credit rating, industry, and geographic region, as well as an assessment of the potential impact of macroeconomic trends on the
portfolio’s risk profile. The report also includes a summary of all active exceptions and their status.
Business units are required to prepare and submit a monthly Tenor Monitoring Report to CRM, detailing the status of all credit
facilities within their portfolios, including their remaining tenors, any early warning signs of credit deterioration, and the results of
their ongoing monitoring activities. This information is used by CRM to update the bank’s central credit registry and to identify
any emerging risks.
The GCC receives a dedicated Tenor Limit Exception Report on a quarterly basis, which provides a detailed review of all
exceptions approved during the quarter, including the rationale for each exception, the mitigating factors, and the enhanced
monitoring and reporting requirements. The report also includes an assessment of the overall effectiveness of the tenor limit
policy and any recommendations for improvements or adjustments."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:47:218> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 218" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:47> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "94dc4af6acabbacb87abaf31760cbf7b02d3c70b267457e9f40c7c06d0459c5a"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 4.2.9 Reporting
Reporting is a vital component of the bank’s country risk management framework, providing transparency and accountability to
senior management, the Board, and regulators. The bank’s reporting requirements are designed to ensure that all stakeholders
have timely and accurate information on the bank’s country risk exposures, the effectiveness of its risk management practices,
and any emerging risks or breaches. This section outlines the key reports, their frequency, content, and distribution.
The primary country risk report is the Country Risk Exposure Report (CRER), which is generated monthly and distributed to the
RMC, the CRO, the CCO, and the heads of all business lines. The CRER provides a comprehensive overview of the bank’s
country risk exposures, including aggregate exposure by country, CRR, and sector, as well as a comparison against the
established quantitative limits. The report highlights any breaches of limits, approved exceptions, and the status of remediation
plans for resolving breaches. It also includes a summary of recent changes in CRRs and any significant political or economic
developments that may impact the bank’s country risk profile.
In addition to the CRER, the CRM unit produces a Country Risk Assessment Report (CRAR) on a quarterly basis. The CRAR
provides a deeper analysis of the bank’s country risk exposures, including trends in CRRs, the performance of the bank’s
country risk models, and the results of stress tests and scenario analyses. The report also includes a review of the bank’s
country risk appetite and any recommendations for adjustments to the policy or limits. The CRAR is presented to the RMC and
the Board, and its findings are incorporated into the bank’s broader risk management discussions.
The bank also prepares ad-hoc reports in response to specific requests from senior management, the Board, or regulators.
These reports may focus on particular countries, sectors, or risk factors, and are tailored to address the specific concerns or
questions raised. For example, a regulator may request a detailed analysis of the bank’s exposures to a country experiencing
political unrest, including the bank’s assessment of the risk and its mitigation strategies. Ad-hoc reports are prepared by the
CRM unit in collaboration with the relevant business lines and other stakeholders, as needed.
All country risk reports are subject to independent validation by the bank’s Internal Audit function, which reviews the accuracy,
completeness, and timeliness of the reports. Internal Audit also assesses whether the reports provide sufficient information to
support informed decision-making by senior management and the Board. The findings of these validations are documented in
Internal Audit reports, which are submitted to the RMC and the Board. Any deficiencies identified in the reporting process must
be addressed through a corrective action plan, with the CRM unit responsible for implementing the necessary improvements."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:67:1362> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1362" ;
