    cpi:source_checksum "d1896594bbb1e031524110785eaf09776756cb1b2a0a57e8c67c52264103b881"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text "Case history, collection efforts, insolvency, collateral, legal status and recovery forecast shall support the decision."^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:73:1633> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1633" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:73> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "35"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f55b2599f076c2c3cd110aa13a8fa88abde99e1a4028d824284dba40e3093b68"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance
The governance framework for this Policy is designed to ensure clear accountability, effective oversight, and robust control
mechanisms for liquidity risk management within the credit portfolio. Ultimate responsibility for the Policy rests with the Board of
Directors (the 'Board'), which delegates operational oversight to ALCO. ALCO is chaired by the Chief Financial Officer (CFO)
and includes the CRO, Chief Credit Officer (CCO), and heads of key business units.
ALCO is responsible for approving the Policy, monitoring its effectiveness, and ensuring compliance with its provisions. It meets
quarterly to review liquidity risk exposures, assess the adequacy of liquidity buffers, and evaluate the Bank’s adherence to the
Policy’s thresholds and controls. ALCO may convene ad hoc meetings in response to significant liquidity events or emerging
risks. Decisions made by ALCO are documented in meeting minutes and communicated to relevant stakeholders.
The CRO, in collaboration with the CCO, is responsible for the day-to-day implementation of this Policy. This includes
developing and maintaining the liquidity risk management framework, setting internal thresholds, and ensuring that business
units adhere to the Policy’s requirements. The CRO also has the authority to impose additional controls or restrictions on credit
activities that pose elevated liquidity risks, subject to ALCO approval.
The Credit Risk Management (CRM) department, under the direction of the CCO, is tasked with monitoring the liquidity
characteristics of the credit portfolio. This includes assessing the liquidity risk of individual exposures, aggregating portfolio-level
liquidity metrics, and reporting findings to ALCO and the CRO. CRM works closely with the Treasury department to ensure that
liquidity risk is managed in a coordinated manner across the Bank.
Internal Audit conducts independent reviews of the Policy’s effectiveness at least annually. These reviews assess the adequacy
of controls, the accuracy of reporting, and the compliance of business units with the Policy’s provisions. Findings from Internal
Audit are reported to the Audit Committee of the Board, which ensures that any identified deficiencies are addressed promptly.
External auditors may also evaluate the Policy as part of their broader assessment of the Bank’s risk management framework."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1563> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1563" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Monitoring and Trigger Events
The monitoring framework specified in RAT-1.0 is designed to ensure that credit exposures are continuously assessed for
changes in risk profile, and that appropriate actions are taken in response to adverse developments. The bank employs a
combination of automated surveillance tools and manual reviews to monitor its credit portfolio, with the frequency and intensity
of monitoring proportional to the risk rating of the exposure.
All credit exposures are subject to automated monitoring on a daily basis, with the bank’s Risk Monitoring team tracking key
financial and non-financial indicators for signs of deterioration. These indicators include financial ratio trends, payment behavior,
credit rating changes, and adverse news events. Automated alerts are generated when predefined thresholds are breached,
such as a decline in the counterparty’s interest coverage ratio below 1.5x or a downgrade in its external credit rating.
In addition to automated monitoring, the bank conducts periodic manual reviews of its credit exposures. Investment-grade
exposures (rated BBB- or higher) are reviewed at least annually, while sub-investment-grade exposures (rated BB+ or lower)
are reviewed at least quarterly. Retail exposures are subject to ongoing behavioral monitoring, with individual accounts flagged
for review if they exhibit delinquency, over-limit usage, or other signs of stress.
Trigger events are defined as specific occurrences that necessitate an immediate review of the credit exposure. These include,
but are not limited to, a breach of financial covenants, a payment default, a material adverse change in the counterparty’s
financial condition, or a significant negative news event (e.g., regulatory action, litigation, or a natural disaster affecting the
counterparty’s operations). Upon the occurrence of a trigger event, the responsible relationship manager must initiate a review
within 5 business days and submit a recommendation to the appropriate approval authority within 10 business days.
The results of monitoring activities and trigger event reviews are documented in the bank’s Credit Monitoring System, with all
actions and rationales recorded for audit purposes. The Risk Monitoring team provides monthly reports to the CRM department
and the CRO, summarizing the number and nature of alerts, the outcomes of reviews, and any emerging trends or concerns.
The internal audit function conducts semi-annual reviews of the monitoring framework to assess its effectiveness and identify
any gaps or weaknesses."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:146> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 146" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1.1.1 Purpose and Applicability
This clause, 1.1 Enterprise Limits, establishes the foundational framework for aggregate credit exposure management across
the institution, ensuring alignment with the overarching principles of Parent Policy RAF-2026-v1.0. The purpose of this
document is to define the quantitative and qualitative boundaries within which credit risk exposures must be maintained to
preserve capital adequacy, liquidity stability, and compliance with regulatory expectations. As a synthetic demonstration
document, all thresholds, roles, and processes herein are illustrative and designed for training, testing, or system validation
purposes only.
The applicability of this clause extends to all legal entities, business lines, and geographic operations under the institution’s
consolidated supervision. It governs all credit exposures, including but not limited to loans, advances, guarantees, derivatives,
and contingent liabilities, regardless of the counterparty type (e.g., corporate, commercial, retail, or sovereign). Exclusions are
explicitly documented in Section 1.1.2 and must be reviewed annually to ensure continued relevance.
The policy is grounded in the principle of risk-based limits, where exposure thresholds are calibrated based on the
creditworthiness of the counterparty, the economic sector, and the jurisdiction of exposure. Synthetic thresholds in this
document are set at conservative levels to demonstrate robust risk management practices. For example, the aggregate
exposure to any single corporate group is capped at 15% of Tier 1 capital, while sectoral concentrations are limited to 25% of
total risk-weighted assets (RWA).
Compliance with this clause is mandatory for all personnel involved in credit origination, approval, monitoring, and reporting.
Non-adherence to the limits or processes outlined herein may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination, as
well as potential regulatory sanctions. The synthetic nature of this document does not absolve users of their responsibility to
treat it as a binding policy during demonstrations or simulations.
This clause is effective as of September 10, 2026, and supersedes all prior versions of enterprise limit frameworks. It is
intended to be read in conjunction with Parent Policy RAF-2026-v1.0, which provides the strategic context and high-level risk
appetite statements. Any inconsistencies between this clause and the parent policy must be escalated to the Chief Risk Officer
(CRO) for resolution."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:29:148> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 148" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:29> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "6b3bf9c190ff659c1ac64106df816991db4e26223717c8c98feeea2addefa70c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 1.1.2 Scope and Exclusions
The scope of this clause encompasses all credit exposures that arise from the institution’s banking, trading, and investment
activities, whether on-balance sheet or off-balance sheet. This includes direct lending, syndicated loans, credit derivatives, repo
transactions, and trade finance instruments. The policy applies uniformly across all currencies and jurisdictions, with
adjustments made for local regulatory requirements where necessary.
Exclusions from this clause are limited and explicitly defined to avoid ambiguity. The following exposures are not subject to the
enterprise limits described herein: intra-group exposures between the institution and its wholly owned subsidiaries, exposures
to sovereign entities rated ‘AAA’ or equivalent by at least two of the major credit rating agencies, and exposures fully
guaranteed by a qualifying government or multilateral agency. Additionally, exposures arising from the institution’s proprietary
trading book, as defined in the Market Risk Policy, are excluded from these limits.
Notwithstanding the exclusions, the institution retains the discretion to apply stricter limits or additional controls to any exposure,
regardless of its eligibility for exclusion. This discretion is exercised by the Credit Policy Committee (CPC), which meets
quarterly to review the appropriateness of the scope and exclusions. Decisions of the CPC are documented in the policy
minutes and communicated to all relevant stakeholders within five business days.
The synthetic thresholds and exclusions in this document are designed to reflect a prudent and conservative approach to credit
risk management. For instance, while exposures to ‘AAA’ sovereigns are excluded, the institution may still monitor such
exposures for concentration risk or liquidity purposes. Similarly, intra-group exposures, though excluded, are subject to
separate governance under the Group Treasury Policy.
Any proposed changes to the scope or exclusions must be submitted to the CRO for approval, accompanied by a risk
assessment and business justification. The CRO will consult with the Chief Financial Officer (CFO) and the Chief Compliance
Officer (CCO) before making a determination. Approved changes are incorporated into the policy through a formal amendment
process, with an effective date no earlier than 30 days after approval."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:60:663> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 663" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:60> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "22"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:source_checksum "80db0ab3861949d729d9ba87a55ada2de6357e143e6dbb048588441f195532ce"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text "Before production use, replace synthetic roles, formulas, criteria, thresholds, legal references and frequencies with approved values. Changes require impact assessment, approval, versioning, testing, communication and retrieval-index update. Superseded versions remain auditable but shall not be retrieved as current."^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1551> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1551" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance and Roles
The governance framework for RAT-1.0 Credit Rating Standards is designed to ensure accountability, independence, and
oversight in the credit rating process. The Board of Directors retains ultimate responsibility for the approval and periodic review
of this policy, delegating its implementation to the CRO. The CRO, in turn, is responsible for ensuring that the policy is applied
consistently across the bank and that all personnel adhere to its provisions.
The Credit Risk Management (CRM) department is the primary owner of this policy and is tasked with its day-to-day
administration. The CRM department is responsible for developing and maintaining the credit rating methodologies, tools, and
systems used by the bank. It also oversees the training of personnel involved in the rating process and ensures that all ratings
are assigned in accordance with the established criteria. The Head of CRM reports directly to the CRO and provides regular
updates on the performance and effectiveness of the rating standards.
Credit analysts and underwriters are responsible for the execution of the rating process, including the collection and analysis of
relevant data, the application of rating methodologies, and the documentation of rating rationales. These individuals must
possess the necessary qualifications and experience, as defined in the bank’s Competency Framework for Credit
Professionals. Analysts and underwriters are required to complete annual training on the RAT-1.0 standards and any updates
thereto.
The Independent Risk Validation (IRV) team plays a critical role in the governance framework by providing an independent
review of the credit rating process. The IRV team is responsible for validating the accuracy and consistency of ratings,
assessing the effectiveness of the rating methodologies, and identifying any potential biases or weaknesses in the process. The
IRV team reports its findings directly to the CRO and the Board’s Risk Committee, ensuring that any issues are addressed
promptly and transparently.
Escalation paths are clearly defined to resolve disputes or uncertainties in the rating process. If a credit analyst or underwriter
disagrees with a proposed rating, the matter must be escalated to the relevant Credit Committee, which includes senior
representatives from CRM, IRV, and the business unit. The Credit Committee’s decision is final, unless further escalation is
warranted to the CRO. All escalations and their resolutions must be documented in the bank’s Credit Rating Dispute Log."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:72:1619> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1619" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:72> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "34"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "1dd66e05d6be0b8de2ae8d5020a930941c17fa515a794474c89f049c3098bae3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approvals
The approvals process for duration risk is designed to ensure that all material exposures are reviewed and authorized by the
appropriate level of management, in accordance with the Bank’s delegation of authority framework. This section outlines the
roles, responsibilities, and escalation paths for approving credit transactions, limit breaches, and exceptions under this Policy.
All approvals must be documented in the Bank’s credit system and retained for a minimum of 7 years.
For standard credit transactions that comply with the quantitative limits and underwriting criteria set forth in this Policy, approval
authority is delegated to the relevant business unit head or their designated proxy. For example, a term loan to a corporate
borrower with a duration of 5 years and a DSCR of 1.30x under stress scenarios may be approved by the Head of Corporate
Banking, provided that the exposure does not exceed the business unit’s sub-limit. Such approvals must be supported by a
completed credit memo, including the duration risk assessment and sensitivity analysis.
Transactions that exceed the business unit’s sub-limits or the Bank’s transaction-level limits require approval from the RMC. For
instance, a 12-year project finance loan would require RMC approval due to its duration exceeding the 10-year transaction limit.
The business unit must submit a request to the RMC, including a detailed rationale for the transaction, an assessment of the
duration risk, and proposed mitigants (e.g., hedging, shorter amortization periods). The RMC will review the request at its next
scheduled meeting, or earlier if the transaction is time-sensitive.
Exceptions to the Policy’s quantitative limits or underwriting criteria must also be approved by the RMC. An exception may be
granted if the transaction presents a unique opportunity that aligns with the Bank’s strategic objectives, or if the duration risk
can be effectively mitigated through alternative means. For example, the RMC may approve a 6-year loan to a high-quality
borrower in a low-volatility sector, even if the business unit’s sub-limit is 5 years, provided that the loan is hedged with an
interest rate swap. All exceptions must be documented, including the rationale, the mitigants, and the expected duration of the
exception.
In cases where a limit breach occurs due to market movements or other factors outside the Bank’s control, the relevant
business unit must notify Risk Management within 24 hours. Risk Management will then assess the breach and determine
whether it can be resolved through portfolio rebalancing or whether an exception is required. If the breach cannot be resolved
within 5 business days, it must be escalated to the RMC for approval of a temporary exception. The RMC will review the breach
at each subsequent meeting until it is resolved."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:policyclass:2d118e50d28316552944> a cpi:PolicyClass,
        cpi:TaxonomyConcept ;
    rdfs:label "Purpose and objectives" ;
