<urn:cpi:source:48:238> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 238" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting and Documentation
Reporting on coverage ratios is a key component of the bank’s credit risk management and regulatory compliance framework.
The Credit Monitoring team is responsible for generating monthly reports on coverage ratio performance across the portfolio.
These reports include a summary of ratio trends, breaches, exceptions, and corrective actions, and are distributed to the CCO,
CRC, and relevant business unit heads.
The policy requires that all coverage ratio calculations and supporting documentation be retained in the borrower’s credit file.
This includes financial statements, valuation reports, and any other data used to calculate the ratios. The credit file must also
include a record of all approvals, exceptions, and waivers related to coverage ratios, as well as any corrective actions or
remediation plans.
In addition to internal reporting, the bank is required to report coverage ratio data to regulators as part of its periodic filings. For
example, the bank may be required to report the distribution of DSCR and ICR values across its portfolio, as well as the number
and value of loans with coverage ratio breaches. The Regulatory Reporting team is responsible for ensuring that all regulatory
reports are accurate, complete, and submitted on time.
The policy also requires that the bank conduct an annual review of its coverage ratio reporting processes to ensure their
continued effectiveness. This review includes an assessment of the accuracy and timeliness of ratio calculations, the adequacy
of documentation, and the effectiveness of escalation and approval processes. Findings from the review are presented to the
CRC and the bank’s Board of Directors, with corrective actions tracked to closure.
This section of the synthetic policy demonstrates the bank’s commitment to transparency and accountability in credit risk
reporting. The reporting and documentation requirements outlined herein are illustrative and designed to ensure that coverage
ratio data is accurately captured, monitored, and reported. Actual reporting practices may vary based on the bank’s regulatory
obligations and internal governance standards."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:50:276> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 276" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:50> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "8"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 8 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """8. 8. Exceptions
Exceptions to the haircut policy may be granted in limited circumstances, provided that they are justified, documented, and
approved through the proper channels. The bank recognizes that rigid adherence to standard haircuts may not always be
practical or in the best interest of the bank and its clients. However, exceptions are closely scrutinized to ensure they do not
introduce unacceptable risk.
The most common basis for an exception is the unique characteristics of the collateral or the transaction. For example, a
borrower may pledge a highly specialized asset with a stable value, such as a rare piece of machinery with a ready buyer. In
such cases, the credit officer may propose a lower haircut, supported by evidence of the asset’s liquidity and value stability.
To request an exception, the credit officer must submit a formal exception request to the Collateral Management Unit (CMU).
The request must include a detailed rationale for the exception, supporting evidence (e.g., independent valuations, market
data), and an assessment of the incremental risk to the bank. The CMU reviews the request and may approve it if the
justification is compelling and the risk is acceptable.
Exceptions that involve a haircut reduction of more than 5 percentage points or a collateral value exceeding USD 5 million
require approval from the Credit Risk Committee (CRC). The CRC evaluates the request based on the bank’s risk appetite, the
potential impact on the loan portfolio, and the precedents set by previous exceptions. The CRC’s decision is final and must be
documented in the exception log.
All approved exceptions are subject to enhanced monitoring, including more frequent revaluations and stress testing. The CMU
tracks exceptions in a centralized log, which is reviewed quarterly by the CRC to ensure that the bank’s exposure to
non-standard haircuts remains within acceptable limits. This section is part of a synthetic demonstration dataset."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:62:790> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 790" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:62> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "24"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
