<urn:cpi:source:48:226> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 226" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance and Roles
Governance of the Coverage Ratio Policy is vested in the Credit Risk Committee (CRC), which is responsible for its approval,
periodic review, and amendments. The CRC delegates operational oversight to the Chief Credit Officer (CCO), who ensures
adherence to the policy across all business units. The CCO is empowered to issue clarifications, temporary waivers, or
adjustments to ratio thresholds, provided such actions are documented and reported to the CRC within 30 days.
The Head of Underwriting is responsible for ensuring that all credit applications comply with the coverage ratio requirements
outlined in this policy. Underwriters must validate ratio calculations, verify the accuracy of input data, and escalate any
discrepancies to the Credit Risk team. Failure to adhere to these requirements may result in the rejection of the credit
application or disciplinary action, as outlined in the bank’s Code of Conduct.
The Credit Risk team, led by the Director of Credit Risk, is tasked with monitoring the performance of coverage ratios across the
portfolio. This includes conducting periodic audits of ratio calculations, assessing the impact of macroeconomic changes on
ratio thresholds, and recommending policy adjustments to the CRC. The team must also ensure that all exceptions to the policy
are properly documented, justified, and approved through the escalation paths defined in Section 9.
Independent validation of coverage ratio calculations is performed by the Internal Audit team on an annual basis. The audit
scope includes a sample of credit files, with a focus on high-risk exposures or transactions where exceptions have been
granted. Findings from these audits are presented to the CRC and the bank’s Board of Directors, with corrective actions tracked
to closure. This governance structure is designed to ensure the integrity and effectiveness of the policy.
This synthetic governance framework is intended to illustrate the roles and responsibilities typical of a large financial institution.
In practice, the bank’s actual governance structure may vary based on its organizational size, complexity, and regulatory
environment. This section underscores the importance of clear accountability and separation of duties in credit risk
management."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:54:355> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 355" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:54> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "7"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "16"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "350cf7f7d62e85428cf6a9698bfd3ab07d8e9c57b05722882ba5e0af64e6dcab"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """7. 7. Monitoring and Controls
Ongoing monitoring of construction loans is critical to identifying and addressing risks in a timely manner. The bank requires
monthly progress reports from the borrower, which must include updated construction budgets, disbursement requests, and
milestone achievements. These reports are reviewed by the Relationship Manager and forwarded to the Construction Risk
Officer (CRO) for validation.
In addition to borrower-provided reports, the bank conducts independent site inspections at least quarterly for all active
construction loans. Inspections are performed by third-party engineers approved by the bank and focus on verifying the physical
progress of the project, the quality of construction, and compliance with building codes and permits. Inspection reports are
submitted to the CRO and retained in the loan file.
The bank employs a series of early warning indicators to proactively identify potential issues. These include: (1) disbursement
requests exceeding 110% of the approved budget for any line item; (2) delays in achieving construction milestones by more
than 30 days; (3) a decline in the borrower’s credit rating or financial condition; or (4) adverse market conditions, such as a 10%
or greater decline in local real estate prices. Triggering any of these indicators requires immediate escalation to the CRO.
Controls are also in place to manage disbursements. Loan funds are disbursed in accordance with a pre-approved draw
schedule, which is tied to the achievement of specific construction milestones. Each disbursement request must be
accompanied by lien waivers from contractors and suppliers, as well as evidence of payment for prior disbursements. The bank
will not release funds for any milestone until all prior milestones have been completed and documented to the bank’s
satisfaction.
The CRO conducts a comprehensive portfolio review on a quarterly basis, which includes an assessment of each construction
loan’s performance against its approved budget, timeline, and financial projections. Loans identified as high-risk are subject to
enhanced monitoring, including more frequent inspections and reporting. The results of the portfolio review are presented to the
CLC and documented in a report to the CCO."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1558> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1558" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
RAT-1.0 establishes quantitative limits and thresholds to ensure that credit exposures are managed within the bank’s risk
appetite and regulatory constraints. These limits are designed to prevent excessive concentration of risk and to ensure that the
bank maintains adequate capital and liquidity buffers. The thresholds are applied at various levels, including individual
counterparties, industry sectors, geographic regions, and the overall portfolio.
For individual counterparties, the bank sets exposure limits based on the assigned credit rating. The maximum exposure to a
single counterparty is capped at 25% of the bank’s Tier 1 capital for investment-grade counterparties (rated BBB- or higher) and
10% of Tier 1 capital for sub-investment-grade counterparties (rated BB+ or lower). Exposures to retail counterparties are
subject to aggregate limits, with no single retail exposure exceeding 1% of Tier 1 capital.
Industry sector limits are established to prevent over-concentration in any single sector. The bank’s exposure to any one
industry sector may not exceed 20% of its total credit exposures. Sectors are defined using the Global Industry Classification
Standard (GICS), and exposures are aggregated at the two-digit GICS level. Geographic limits are also applied, with no single
country (excluding the bank’s home country) accounting for more than 15% of total credit exposures.
The bank also imposes internal risk-based limits, which are more granular than the regulatory limits. These include limits on the
proportion of the portfolio that can be assigned to each rating grade. For example, the bank targets that no more than 10% of its
total credit exposures should be rated BB+ or lower. Additionally, the bank sets thresholds for watchlist and non-performing
exposures, with the latter not exceeding 3% of total exposures at any time.
Quantitative limits are monitored on a daily basis by the bank’s Risk Monitoring team, which reports any breaches to the CRM
department and the relevant business units. Breaches must be addressed within 30 days, either by reducing the exposure or
obtaining a waiver from the CRO. All waivers must be documented and justified, with a clear action plan for remediation. The
bank’s internal audit function conducts quarterly reviews of limit compliance and breach management processes."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

