<urn:cpi:source:53:333> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 333" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:53> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "15"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b80889a26cb577a996729bcf2be14b0134ee6688799b0a90edb704a5bd0b1958"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approvals
The approval process for CRE loans under this synthetic policy is designed to ensure that all transactions comply with the
bank’s underwriting criteria, sub-sector limits, and risk appetite. Approval authority is delegated based on loan size, complexity,
and risk profile, with higher-risk or larger loans requiring approval from more senior levels of management.
Loans with an outstanding balance of up to $5 million may be approved by a designated CRE Underwriting Manager, provided
they comply with all policy criteria, including sub-sector limits. Loans between $5 million and $20 million require approval from
the Regional Credit Officer (RCO), while loans exceeding $20 million must be approved by the Chief Credit Officer (CCO) or
their delegate.
Loans that would cause the bank’s exposure to a sub-sector to exceed its quantitative limit require approval from the CRE Risk
Management Team, in addition to the standard underwriting approval. The CRE Risk Management Team must conduct a
thorough review of the loan’s risk profile, the current exposure to the sub-sector, and the potential impact on the bank’s
portfolio. If the loan is approved, it must be classified as an exception and reported to the Credit Risk Committee.
The approval process also includes a secondary review for loans with complex structures, such as those involving multiple
properties, cross-collateralization, or non-recourse financing. These loans must be reviewed by the CRE Risk Management
Team, regardless of size, to ensure compliance with the policy and to assess any additional risks.
All approvals must be documented in the bank’s loan origination system, with clear evidence of compliance with the policy’s
criteria. Approval records must include the names and titles of the approving authorities, the date of approval, and any
conditions or stipulations attached to the approval. This documentation is subject to internal audit and regulatory review."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:73:1635> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1635" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:73> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "35"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f55b2599f076c2c3cd110aa13a8fa88abde99e1a4028d824284dba40e3093b68"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The Bank’s credit underwriting process incorporates liquidity risk considerations to ensure that new exposures align with the
liquidity risk appetite defined in this Policy. All credit applications must include a liquidity risk assessment, which evaluates the
potential impact of the exposure on the Bank’s liquidity profile under normal and stressed conditions. This assessment is
conducted in parallel with the traditional credit risk analysis and is a mandatory component of the underwriting decision.
Liquidity risk assessments for credit exposures must consider the following factors: the borrower’s ability to generate cash flows
to service debt, the liquidity of any collateral pledged, the maturity profile of the exposure, and the potential for early repayment
or default. For exposures with embedded options, such as revolving credit facilities, the assessment must also evaluate the
likelihood of drawdowns and the Bank’s ability to fund such drawdowns without disrupting its liquidity position.
The underwriting criteria specify that no single credit exposure may exceed 5% of the Bank’s total liquid assets, as defined in
Section 6 (Quantitative Limits), unless approved by ALCO. For exposures exceeding 2% of total liquid assets, enhanced
liquidity risk assessments are required, including scenario analysis and stress testing. These assessments must be
documented and reviewed by CRM before the exposure is approved.
Collateral accepted for credit exposures must meet liquidity criteria established by the Bank. Highly liquid collateral, such as
cash or government securities, is preferred, while illiquid collateral, such as real estate or private equity, is subject to haircuts
and additional monitoring. The liquidity of collateral is reassessed at least annually or upon the occurrence of a material event,
such as a downgrade in the borrower’s credit rating or a change in market conditions.
The underwriting process also considers the diversification of the credit portfolio from a liquidity perspective. Concentrations in
illiquid sectors, geographies, or asset classes are monitored and controlled to ensure that the Bank’s liquidity profile remains
balanced. The CCO, in consultation with CRM, may impose limits on exposures to specific sectors or asset classes if they are
deemed to pose excessive liquidity risk."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:riskmetric:d1b222d1e468ecab93d8> a cpi:RiskAttribute,
        cpi:RiskMetric ;
    rdfs:label "Interest Coverage Ratio" ;
    cpi:BINDS_TO_ATTRIBUTE <urn:cpi:dataattribute:07e7432d1082e1759f19> ;
    cpi:allowed_units "x"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:metric_key "interest_coverage_ratio"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:sourceRef <urn:cpi:source:1:8>,
        <urn:cpi:source:48:224>,
        <urn:cpi:source:48:230>,
        <urn:cpi:source:56:392>,
        <urn:cpi:source:69:1563> ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true .

<urn:cpi:source:49:246> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 246" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:49> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "11"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "067ea0df55a1950a6f414e2436d7ac36cad81a25ad50f752f9ba8449ed70a5eb"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance
Governance for the Eligible Collateral clause is overseen by the bank’s Credit Risk Committee (CRC), which is responsible for
approving and periodically reviewing the policy to ensure its continued relevance and effectiveness. The CRC delegates
operational oversight to the Collateral Management Unit (CMU), a dedicated team within the Risk Management Division. This
structure ensures clear accountability and separation of duties.
The CMU is tasked with implementing the policy, developing procedural guidelines, and providing training to relevant staff. It
also monitors compliance with the policy and reports any breaches or exceptions to the CRC. The CMU works closely with
Legal, Compliance, and Internal Audit to ensure that the policy aligns with regulatory requirements and the bank’s internal
controls. This clause, being synthetic, demonstrates how governance structures can be documented for clarity and
accountability.
The bank’s Board of Directors retains ultimate responsibility for the policy’s adherence to the bank’s risk appetite and strategic
objectives. The Board receives regular updates from the CRC on the performance of the collateral portfolio, including trends in
collateral types, valuation adjustments, and any emerging risks. These updates are typically provided quarterly, with ad-hoc
reports generated as needed for significant events or regulatory changes.
Escalation paths are clearly defined within the governance framework. For instance, any disputes regarding collateral eligibility
or valuation must be escalated to the CMU for resolution. If the dispute remains unresolved, it is further escalated to the CRC.
The policy also mandates that all exceptions to the collateral eligibility criteria be documented and approved by the appropriate
authority, with higher-risk exceptions requiring CRC approval.
The governance framework includes regular audits of the collateral management process, conducted by Internal Audit at least
annually. These audits assess compliance with the policy, the accuracy of collateral valuations, and the effectiveness of
controls. Findings from these audits are reported to the CRC and the Board, with corrective actions tracked to completion."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:57:422> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 422" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:57> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "19"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "0171f0f0bb413e95a3c9a8253d8c5f71b9496025d505b0e93709b7366b224fc5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Amendments
This policy is subject to regular review to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with evolving regulatory
requirements and industry best practices. The policy mandates that a comprehensive review be conducted at least annually,
with the involvement of the Credit Risk Department (CRD), the Risk Management Committee (RMC), and other relevant
stakeholders. The review process includes an assessment of the policy’s effectiveness in managing RWA, as well as an
evaluation of any emerging risks or regulatory changes that may impact the bank’s RWA treatment processes.
The annual review is led by the CRD, which is responsible for coordinating the input of all relevant stakeholders and producing
a review report. The report includes an analysis of the policy’s performance against its objectives, a summary of any material
changes in the regulatory or operating environment, and recommendations for amendments or enhancements. The review
report is submitted to the RMC for approval, and any proposed amendments are presented to the Board of Directors for final
approval.
In addition to the annual review, the policy may be amended on an ad-hoc basis in response to significant regulatory changes,
emerging risks, or strategic initiatives. Ad-hoc amendments are typically initiated by the CRD or the RMC and are subject to the
same approval process as the annual review. The policy requires that all amendments be documented in a change log, which is
maintained in the bank’s central repository and includes a summary of the changes, the rationale for the amendments, and the
approval date.
The policy also mandates that the bank conduct a post-implementation review (PIR) for any material changes to its RWA
treatment methodologies or systems. The PIR is conducted within six months of the implementation date and includes an
assessment of the changes’ effectiveness in achieving their intended objectives, as well as an evaluation of any unintended
consequences. The results of the PIR are documented in a report, which is submitted to the RMC for review.
This synthetic section on policy review and amendments illustrates how a bank might ensure that its RWA treatment policy
remains up-to-date and effective in a dynamic regulatory and operating environment. It includes fictional review frequencies,
roles, and processes to demonstrate the importance of regular and comprehensive policy reviews in maintaining compliance
with Basel IV and internal governance standards. All review and amendment processes described herein are intended for
demonstration purposes only."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "Basel IV"^^xsd:string .

