<urn:cpi:source:52:321> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 321" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:52> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "a31614bc9583cac5c205cf3ac248b30964fb2ecb3c011a9d90d81ca1e5d5d963"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Maintenance
The Spread Grid is subject to periodic review to ensure that it remains relevant, effective, and aligned with the bank’s risk
appetite and regulatory expectations. The CRM department conducts a comprehensive review of the grid at least annually, with
input from business units, risk analytics, compliance, and Internal Audit. The review assesses the grid’s performance in
predicting credit losses, its consistency with market practices, and its compliance with regulatory requirements.
The annual review includes a validation of the quantitative inputs and thresholds used in the grid. The CRM department tests
the grid’s calibration by comparing actual default rates and loss severities to the predicted PD and LGD values. If the grid is
found to be miscalibrated (e.g., actual losses exceed expected losses for a given risk bucket), the thresholds or inputs are
adjusted accordingly. The review also considers feedback from underwriters and business units on the practicality and usability
of the grid.
Ad hoc reviews are conducted in response to material changes in the bank’s operating environment. For example, a significant
economic downturn, a new regulatory capital framework, or a merger or acquisition may necessitate an interim review of the
grid. The CPC has the authority to mandate such reviews and to fast-track updates to the grid if warranted. All ad hoc reviews
must be completed within 90 days of the triggering event.
Updates to the Spread Grid are approved by the CPC and communicated to all stakeholders through formal policy memos,
training sessions, and updates to the bank’s policy management system. The CRM department is responsible for ensuring that
all business units and risk functions are aware of the changes and have the necessary tools and training to implement them.
The effective date of updates is typically 30 days after approval, allowing time for systems updates and staff training.
The bank maintains a version control system for the Spread Grid, with each update assigned a unique version number and
effective date. The system tracks all changes, including the rationale for updates, the stakeholders consulted, and the approvals
obtained. This ensures transparency and accountability in the policy maintenance process. The current version of the Spread
Grid, as demonstrated in this synthetic document, is PRC-v1.7, Clause 1.2, effective as of the date of this policy."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1561> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1561" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 7 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1 million and USD 10 million require the approval of the Head of the relevant business unit, while exposures between USD 10
million and USD 50 million must be approved by the regional Credit Committee.
Exposures exceeding USD 50 million, or those involving sub-investment-grade counterparties (rated BB+ or lower), require the
approval of the Global Credit Committee, which is chaired by the CRO. The Global Credit Committee also reviews and
approves all exposures that breach the bank’s internal risk-based limits or involve new or complex products. The committee
meets weekly to review pending approvals and is empowered to reject or modify any proposal that does not meet the bank’s
risk standards.
Delegation of approval authority is permitted under specific circumstances, such as the temporary absence of a designated
approver or the need to expedite a time-sensitive transaction. Delegation must be formally documented and approved by the
CRO or their delegate. The delegated authority is limited to a maximum of 30 days and may not be further delegated. All
delegations must be recorded in the bank’s Delegation of Authority Log, which is reviewed quarterly by the internal audit
function.
In cases where a credit exposure requires approval from multiple authorities (e.g., due to its size, risk rating, or complexity), the
most senior authority’s approval is required. For example, a USD 60 million exposure to a BB-rated counterparty would require
approval from both the regional Credit Committee and the Global Credit Committee. The approval process must be completed
within 10 business days of submission, unless an extension is granted by the CRO. All approvals and rejections must be
documented in the bank’s Credit Approval System, with clear rationales provided for any deviations from the standard criteria."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

