<urn:cpi:source:48:228> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 228" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria for Coverage Ratios
Underwriting criteria for coverage ratios are designed to ensure that borrowers demonstrate sufficient financial strength to
service their debt obligations. The primary ratios—DSCR, ICR, and CCR—must meet or exceed the minimum thresholds
specified in Section 2. For term loans, the DSCR must be calculated over the entire loan term, while for revolving facilities, it
must be assessed on a rolling 12-month basis. Underwriters are required to use the bank’s standardized financial models to
perform these calculations.
In addition to meeting the minimum thresholds, underwriters must evaluate the stability and predictability of the borrower’s cash
flows. For example, borrowers in cyclical industries, such as commodities or retail, may be subject to higher DSCR thresholds
or additional covenants to account for revenue volatility. Similarly, borrowers with a high proportion of variable-rate debt may be
required to maintain a higher ICR to mitigate interest rate risk.
Collateral coverage is a critical component of the underwriting process for secured loans. The CCR must be calculated using
the most recent independent valuation of the collateral, with haircuts applied as specified in Section 2. Underwriters must also
assess the liquidity of the collateral and the legal enforceability of the bank’s security interest. For example, real estate collateral
must be insured, and the bank must hold a first-lien position to qualify for the standard CCR threshold.
Underwriters are required to document their rationale for any coverage ratio that falls below the minimum threshold but is still
deemed acceptable. This documentation must include a detailed analysis of mitigating factors, such as strong sponsorship,
additional guarantees, or structural subordination of other creditors. Such cases must be escalated to the Head of Underwriting
for approval, with further escalation to the CCO if the deviation exceeds 10% of the threshold.
This section of the synthetic policy demonstrates the rigor and granularity of underwriting standards in a real-world credit policy.
The criteria outlined herein are illustrative and designed to reflect the bank’s commitment to prudent risk-taking. Actual
underwriting criteria may vary based on the bank’s risk appetite, market conditions, and regulatory requirements."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:72:1611> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1611" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:72> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "34"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "1dd66e05d6be0b8de2ae8d5020a930941c17fa515a794474c89f049c3098bae3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope
This Policy applies to all business units, subsidiaries, and legal entities within the Bank that engage in credit-related activities
with material duration risk exposure. This includes, but is not limited to, corporate lending, commercial real estate financing,
project finance, and fixed-income securities trading. The Policy is designed to be comprehensive, covering both
on-balance-sheet and off-balance-sheet exposures where duration risk is a material consideration.
The scope of this Policy explicitly excludes retail lending products with durations of less than 1 year, as these are deemed to
have immaterial duration risk under normal market conditions. However, exceptions may be made for retail portfolios with
embedded options or structured features that introduce duration risk, such as long-term mortgages with prepayment options. In
such cases, the relevant business unit must seek approval from the Risk Management Committee (RMC) to include these
products within the Policy’s scope.
For the purposes of this synthetic demonstration, the Policy defines duration risk as the potential for losses arising from
changes in interest rates that affect the present value of the Bank’s credit assets and liabilities. This includes both direct interest
rate risk (e.g., changes in benchmark rates) and indirect risks (e.g., spread widening due to credit deterioration). The Policy also
addresses the liquidity implications of duration mismatches, such as the inability to roll over short-term funding for long-term
assets.
The Policy’s application is global, encompassing all jurisdictions in which the Bank operates. However, local regulatory
requirements may necessitate adjustments to certain thresholds or processes. In such instances, the local Chief Risk Officer
(CRO) is responsible for ensuring compliance with both this Policy and applicable local regulations. Any deviations from the
global standards outlined in this Policy must be documented and approved by the Group CRO.
To ensure clarity, this Policy provides a non-exhaustive list of products and activities in scope, including term loans, bonds,
asset-backed securities, and derivatives used for duration hedging. Synthetic examples of out-of-scope items include overnight
repos, demand deposits, and trade finance facilities. The Bank’s Risk Taxonomy Document, referenced in RAF-DUR-v1.0,
provides further detail on the classification of products for duration risk purposes."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:48:240> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 240" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Amendments
The Coverage Ratio Policy is subject to periodic review to ensure its continued relevance and effectiveness in managing credit
risk. The CRC is responsible for conducting a comprehensive review of the policy at least annually, or more frequently if
significant changes occur in the bank’s risk appetite, regulatory environment, or market conditions. The review process includes
an assessment of the policy’s alignment with industry best practices and the bank’s strategic objectives.
Amendments to the policy may be proposed by the CCO, the Head of Underwriting, or the Director of Credit Risk, and must be
approved by the CRC. Proposed amendments are evaluated based on their impact on the bank’s risk profile, operational
complexity, and regulatory compliance. Significant amendments, such as changes to minimum ratio thresholds or approval
authorities, may also require approval from the bank’s Board of Directors.
The policy review process includes a consultation with key stakeholders, such as business unit heads, risk managers, and
compliance officers. Feedback from these stakeholders is incorporated into the policy amendments to ensure that the revised
policy is practical, effective, and aligned with the needs of the business. The CRC is responsible for documenting the rationale
for all amendments and communicating them to the relevant parties.
In addition to periodic reviews, the policy is subject to ad hoc amendments in response to specific events or developments. For
example, the CRC may propose an amendment to the policy in response to a significant change in macroeconomic conditions,
such as a recession or a spike in interest rates. Ad hoc amendments follow the same approval process as periodic
amendments, ensuring that all changes are thoroughly reviewed and authorized.
This synthetic review framework illustrates the bank’s commitment to maintaining a dynamic and responsive credit policy. The
review and amendment processes outlined herein are designed to ensure that the policy remains effective in managing credit
risk while adapting to changes in the bank’s internal and external environment. Actual review practices may vary based on the
bank’s governance structure and regulatory requirements."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:72:1615> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1615" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:72> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "34"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "1dd66e05d6be0b8de2ae8d5020a930941c17fa515a794474c89f049c3098bae3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
Underwriting criteria for duration risk are designed to ensure that new credit exposures are consistent with the Bank’s risk
appetite and the limits established in this Policy. All credit applications must include a duration risk assessment, which
evaluates the potential impact of interest rate changes on the borrower’s ability to service debt and the Bank’s overall portfolio.
This assessment must be documented in the credit memo and approved by the relevant authority as outlined in Section 6
(Approvals).
For term loans and other fixed-rate credit facilities, the underwriting process must incorporate a sensitivity analysis that
measures the change in the present value of the loan under various interest rate scenarios. As a synthetic example, the Bank
may require that the borrower’s debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) remains above 1.20x under a 200-basis-point parallel shift
in the yield curve. Loans that fail to meet this threshold may require additional mitigants, such as shorter durations, floating-rate
structures, or hedging arrangements.
Floating-rate loans are not exempt from duration risk considerations, as they may still expose the Bank to basis risk, spread
risk, or reset risk. For such loans, underwriters must assess the borrower’s ability to absorb higher payments in a rising rate
environment, as well as the potential for margin compression if the Bank’s funding costs increase more rapidly than the loan’s
reference rate. The Policy requires that floating-rate loans include a maximum tenor of 10 years, with reset periods no longer
than 6 months for corporate borrowers.
The underwriting criteria also address the use of derivatives and hedging instruments to manage duration risk. For example, the
Bank may permit the use of interest rate swaps to convert floating-rate assets to fixed-rate or vice versa, provided that the
counterparty risk and collateral requirements are in line with the Bank’s derivatives policy. All hedging strategies must be
approved by the Treasury Risk team and must be documented in the credit file, including the rationale, cost, and expected
effectiveness of the hedge.
In addition to financial metrics, the underwriting process must consider qualitative factors that may influence duration risk, such
as the borrower’s industry, geographic location, and macroeconomic outlook. For instance, loans to borrowers in cyclical
industries (e.g., commodities, shipping) may be subject to stricter duration limits due to the higher volatility of their cash flows.
The Policy requires that such qualitative assessments be supported by market data, internal research, or third-party analyses
and be reviewed by the Risk Management team."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:1:20> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 20" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:1> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "21b6dc2ac5a7b626d6f486564ebfa1ffd0d49d8a47aec79f488f82a87ccfcb8e"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Maintenance
This clause, 4.1 Underwriting Standards, is subject to periodic review to ensure that it remains relevant, effective, and aligned
with the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory expectations. The GCPC is responsible for overseeing the review process, which is
conducted at least annually or more frequently if there are significant changes in the bank’s strategy, risk profile, or the external
environment.
The review process includes an assessment of the policy’s effectiveness in mitigating credit risk, as well as an evaluation of its
consistency with industry best practices and regulatory requirements. The Wholesale Credit Risk Division is responsible for
conducting the review and presenting its findings to the GCPC. The review must include an analysis of credit quality trends, the
frequency and nature of exceptions, and feedback from business lines and independent review functions.
As part of the review, the GCPC may solicit input from external stakeholders, such as regulators, auditors, or industry peers, to
benchmark the bank’s underwriting standards against those of its peers. The GCPC may also commission independent studies
or consultations to identify areas for improvement. The results of the review are documented in a formal report, which includes
recommendations for updates or enhancements to the policy.
Updates to the policy must be approved by the GCPC and communicated to all relevant stakeholders, including relationship
managers, credit analysts, and approval authorities. The updated policy is then incorporated into the bank’s policy management
system, and training is provided to ensure that all personnel are aware of the changes. The effective date of the updated policy
is clearly communicated, and a transition period may be granted for existing exposures to comply with new requirements.
In addition to the annual review, the policy may be updated on an ad hoc basis to address emerging risks or regulatory
changes. For example, if a new regulatory capital framework is introduced, the underwriting standards may need to be adjusted
to reflect the new requirements. Similarly, if the bank identifies a new industry or product type that presents unique risks, the
policy may be updated to include specific underwriting criteria for that segment. All ad hoc updates must follow the same
approval and communication process as the annual review."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:52:311> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 311" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:52> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "a31614bc9583cac5c205cf3ac248b30964fb2ecb3c011a9d90d81ca1e5d5d963"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
The Spread Grid establishes quantitative limits to ensure that credit spreads remain within acceptable ranges based on the
bank’s risk appetite. The base spread for each risk bucket is determined by the borrower’s PD and LGD, as follows: Low Risk
(PD < 1%, LGD < 20%): 100–150 bps; Moderate Risk (PD 1–3%, LGD 20–40%): 150–300 bps; High Risk (PD 3–5%, LGD
40–60%): 300–400 bps; Very High Risk (PD > 5%, LGD > 60%): 400–500 bps. These ranges are synthetic and for
demonstration purposes only.
Adjustments to the base spread are capped to prevent excessive risk-taking or mispricing. For example, qualitative adjustments
for industry risk or management quality cannot exceed ±100 bps for Low and Moderate Risk buckets, ±150 bps for High Risk,
and ±200 bps for Very High Risk. Similarly, collateral adjustments are limited to ±50 bps for scores 1–2 and ±100 bps for scores
3–5. The total spread (base + adjustments) must not fall below the minimum threshold of 100 bps or exceed the maximum
threshold of 700 bps without explicit approval from the CPC.
Tenure adjustments are applied to reflect the increased risk of longer-duration exposures. For example, spreads for exposures
with a tenure of 1–3 years may receive a 0–25 bps adjustment, while those with a tenure of 3–5 years may receive a 25–50 bps
adjustment, and those exceeding 5 years may receive a 50–100 bps adjustment. These adjustments are cumulative with other
qualitative and collateral adjustments but are subject to the same caps.
Currency risk is another critical factor in spread determination. Exposures denominated in currencies with high volatility or
limited liquidity (e.g., emerging market currencies) may receive an additional spread adjustment of up to 50 bps. The CRM
department maintains a list of approved currencies and their associated risk premiums, which is reviewed quarterly.
Transactions in unapproved currencies require CPC approval.
The Spread Grid includes a hard stop rule: any transaction with a total spread exceeding 700 bps must be escalated to the CPC
for review, regardless of the borrower’s risk profile. Similarly, transactions with spreads below 100 bps must be justified by
exceptional collateral quality or other mitigating factors and approved by the Head of Credit Risk. These thresholds are
designed to prevent both excessive risk-taking and uneconomic pricing."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:56:387> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 387" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:56> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "18"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "fd93de0b531f7b06b26a6a55c8dd136cbac2a641dd3fc8ac1611fa9ccfc04995"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance and Authority Framework
The governance framework for credit escalations is structured hierarchically to ensure that decisions are made at the
appropriate level of seniority and expertise. At the base of the hierarchy, Relationship Managers (RMs) are empowered to
approve transactions up to USD 1 million, provided they comply with all standard underwriting criteria and do not involve
high-risk counterparties or jurisdictions. RMs must document their rationale for approval in the bank’s credit management
system (CMS) and retain evidence of compliance with policy requirements.
The next level of authority resides with the Business Unit Credit Officers (BUCOs), who can approve transactions up to USD 5
million. BUCOs are also responsible for reviewing and endorsing or rejecting escalations from RMs for transactions that exceed
the RM’s delegated authority but fall within the BUCO’s limit. BUCOs must conduct an independent assessment of the credit
risk, including a review of the borrower’s financial statements, industry analysis, and collateral valuation, where applicable.
For transactions exceeding USD 5 million but not exceeding USD 25 million, escalation to the Regional Credit Officer (RCO) is
required. The RCO’s approval is also mandatory for any transaction involving a counterparty rated below BB- or domiciled in a
jurisdiction subject to sanctions or heightened AML scrutiny. The RCO must convene a credit review committee (CRC) for
transactions in this range that involve complex structures or significant policy exceptions.
Transactions exceeding USD 25 million, or those involving policy exceptions that cannot be resolved at the RCO level, must be
escalated to the Chief Credit Officer (CCO). The CCO’s approval is final for all credit decisions, except in cases where the
transaction exceeds USD 100 million or involves a new product type not previously approved by the bank. In such instances,
the CCO must seek ratification from the bank’s Board of Directors or its designated Risk Committee.
The authority framework is supported by a system of checks and balances, including independent risk reviews by the bank’s
Risk Management Division (RMD) and compliance attestations by the Legal and Compliance teams. All escalations must be
documented in the CMS, with clear audit trails showing the rationale for decisions, the evidence reviewed, and the identities of
the approving authorities. The Internal Audit function conducts periodic reviews of escalation compliance as part of its broader
mandate to assess the effectiveness of the bank’s credit risk controls."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:56:389> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 389" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:56> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "18"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "fd93de0b531f7b06b26a6a55c8dd136cbac2a641dd3fc8ac1611fa9ccfc04995"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria and Risk Assessment
Underwriting criteria serve as the foundation for determining whether a transaction requires escalation under this policy. The
bank’s standard underwriting framework evaluates counterparties based on five core risk dimensions: financial strength,
industry risk, management quality, collateral coverage, and structural subordination. Each dimension is scored on a scale of 1
to 5, with 1 representing the lowest risk and 5 the highest. A composite risk score is calculated as the weighted average of
these dimensions, with financial strength and industry risk each accounting for 30% of the total score, and the remaining
dimensions accounting for 10% each.
Transactions with a composite risk score of 3.0 or higher must be escalated to the BUCO, regardless of size. Similarly, any
transaction where a single risk dimension scores a 5 must be escalated to the RCO, as this indicates a critical weakness that
could materially impact the bank’s risk exposure. For example, a counterparty with a financial strength score of 5 (e.g., negative
equity or persistent cash flow deficits) would trigger an automatic escalation, even if the other dimensions are scored more
favorably.
In addition to the composite risk score, the bank employs a series of quantitative thresholds to identify transactions requiring
escalation. These include debt service coverage ratios (DSCR) below 1.25x, leverage ratios (total debt to EBITDA) above 4.0x,
and current ratios below 1.0x. Transactions breaching any of these thresholds must be escalated to the next level of authority,
with the escalation path determined by the severity and number of breaches. For instance, a transaction breaching two or more
thresholds would require RCO approval, while a single breach might be addressed at the BUCO level.
Qualitative underwriting criteria also play a critical role in determining escalation requirements. These include assessments of
the borrower’s business model sustainability, competitive positioning, and exposure to environmental, social, and governance
(ESG) risks. For example, a borrower operating in a sector facing significant regulatory disruption (e.g., fossil fuel extraction)
may require escalation to the RCO or CCO, even if its financial metrics are within standard thresholds. Similarly, transactions
involving related-party exposures or complex intercompany guarantees must be escalated to ensure that conflicts of interest are
appropriately managed.
The underwriting criteria are reviewed and updated annually by the Credit Policy Committee (CPC) to reflect changes in market
conditions, regulatory requirements, and the bank’s risk appetite. Any material changes to the criteria must be approved by the
CCO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders through the bank’s policy management system. Relationship Managers
are required to complete annual training on the underwriting framework and pass a competency assessment to retain their
delegated authority."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

