<urn:cpi:source:49:250> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 250" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:49> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "11"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "067ea0df55a1950a6f414e2436d7ac36cad81a25ad50f752f9ba8449ed70a5eb"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
Quantitative limits are established to ensure that the value of collateral is sufficient to cover the credit exposure, even under
stressed conditions. The bank applies a Loan-to-Value (LTV) ratio to determine the maximum credit exposure that can be
secured by a given collateral. For example, residential real estate typically has an LTV limit of 80%, meaning the loan amount
cannot exceed 80% of the property’s appraised value. Commercial real estate may have a lower LTV limit, such as 65%,
reflecting its higher volatility.
Haircuts are applied to the market value of collateral to account for potential declines in value during liquidation. For instance,
publicly traded equities may have a haircut of 20%, meaning only 80% of their market value is considered as eligible collateral.
The haircut percentages are determined based on the asset class, liquidity, and historical volatility, and are reviewed annually
by the CMU. These limits are synthetic but designed to mirror industry standards.
The bank also imposes concentration limits to prevent over-reliance on a single type of collateral or a single borrower. For
example, no more than 15% of the bank’s total collateral portfolio may consist of a single asset class, such as commercial real
estate. Similarly, the bank limits its exposure to any single borrower or group of connected counterparties to 10% of its total
capital base, in line with regulatory requirements.
Dynamic limits are applied to collateral that is subject to significant market fluctuations, such as commodities or foreign
exchange. These limits are adjusted in real-time based on market conditions and are monitored continuously by the CMU. The
bank uses automated systems to track collateral values and trigger margin calls or additional security requirements when
thresholds are breached.
All quantitative limits are documented in the bank’s internal risk management systems and are subject to regular testing and
validation. The CMU conducts back-testing of the limits at least annually to ensure they remain appropriate and effective in
mitigating risk. Any proposed changes to the limits must be approved by the CRC and documented in the policy."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:21:121> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 121" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope
This clause, 3.2 LTV Ceilings, applies to all retail lending products originated, renewed, or refinanced by the bank, including but
not limited to first and second mortgages, home equity lines of credit (HELOCs), auto loans, and secured personal loans. The
scope encompasses all jurisdictions where the bank operates, unless local regulations impose stricter LTV requirements, in
which case the more stringent rule prevails.
The policy is binding for all employees, contractors, and third-party vendors involved in the underwriting, approval, or servicing
of retail loans. This includes loan officers, credit analysts, risk managers, and compliance officers. Adherence to the LTV
ceilings is mandatory, and any deviations must follow the exception process outlined in Section 9 of this document.
For the purposes of this clause, the LTV ratio is defined as the ratio of the loan amount to the appraised value of the collateral,
expressed as a percentage. The appraised value must be determined by an independent, bank-approved valuation method,
such as an automated valuation model (AVM), a licensed appraiser, or a physical inspection, depending on the product type
and loan amount.
This clause does not apply to unsecured lending products, such as credit cards or unsecured personal loans, as these do not
involve collateral. However, for products that may transition from unsecured to secured status (e.g., a personal loan later
secured by an asset), the LTV ceilings must be applied at the time of securing the loan.
The scope also includes periodic reviews of existing loans to ensure continued compliance with LTV ceilings. This is particularly
relevant for loans with long tenors, such as mortgages, where the collateral value may change over time due to market
conditions or property improvements. The bank reserves the right to adjust LTV ceilings for existing loans if risk conditions
warrant such action."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:49:260> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 260" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:49> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "11"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "067ea0df55a1950a6f414e2436d7ac36cad81a25ad50f752f9ba8449ed70a5eb"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Review
The Eligible Collateral clause is subject to periodic review to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with
the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory requirements. The review process is overseen by the CRC, which is responsible for
approving any changes to the policy. The frequency of reviews is determined based on the level of risk and the pace of change
in the bank’s operating environment, with a minimum review cycle of once every two years.
The review process begins with an assessment of the policy’s performance, conducted by the CMU. This assessment includes
an analysis of the collateral portfolio’s performance, the effectiveness of the underwriting criteria and quantitative limits, and the
adequacy of the monitoring and reporting frameworks. The CMU also evaluates the policy’s compliance with regulatory
requirements and industry best practices, using synthetic data to test scenarios and validate assumptions.
Stakeholder feedback is a critical component of the review process. The CMU solicits input from credit officers, risk managers,
legal and compliance teams, and other relevant stakeholders to identify any areas where the policy may be unclear, overly
restrictive, or in need of update. This feedback is gathered through surveys, interviews, and workshops, and is used to inform
the proposed changes to the policy.
Proposed changes to the policy are documented in a review report, which is submitted to the CRC for consideration. The report
includes a summary of the assessment findings, the stakeholder feedback, and the rationale for the proposed changes. The
CRC reviews the report and may request additional information or analysis before approving the changes. Once approved, the
changes are implemented by the CMU, and the updated policy is communicated to all relevant stakeholders.
The review process also includes a retrospective analysis of any exceptions, breaches, or incidents that occurred since the last
review. This analysis evaluates whether the policy’s controls and limits were effective in mitigating risk and whether any
changes are needed to address the root causes of the issues. The findings from this analysis are incorporated into the review
report and are used to inform the proposed changes to the policy. The updated policy is then subject to the next cycle of review,
ensuring continuous improvement and adaptation to the bank’s evolving needs."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:290> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 290" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
This policy establishes the following RAROC floors for the bank’s primary credit asset classes, which serve as the minimum
acceptable thresholds for new originations and portfolio reviews. For Corporate Loans, the RAROC floor is set at 12% for
investment-grade (IG) exposures (rated BBB- or higher) and 18% for non-investment-grade (NIG) exposures (rated BB+ or
lower). Exceptions to these floors require approval from the Head of CRM and the relevant Business Unit Head.
Commercial Real Estate (CRE) exposures are subject to a RAROC floor of 15%, reflecting the higher risk and illiquidity
associated with this asset class. Within CRE, sub-segments such as development loans and speculative construction financing
are assigned a higher floor of 20% due to their elevated risk profiles. The RAROC for CRE exposures must be calculated at
both the individual loan and portfolio levels to ensure compliance with the policy.
For Project Finance, the RAROC floor is set at 16%, accounting for the long-term, non-recourse nature of these exposures and
the complexity of their cash flow structures. Structured Credit Products, including collateralized loan obligations (CLOs) and
credit derivatives, are subject to a floor of 22%, reflecting their leverage, complexity, and potential for tail risk. These thresholds
are applied to both the underlying assets and the tranched structures.
Retail and Small Business Lending portfolios are evaluated on an aggregate basis, with a minimum RAROC floor of 10% for the
entire portfolio. Individual loans within these portfolios are not required to meet the floor, provided the overall portfolio RAROC
exceeds the threshold. However, any sub-portfolio or segment exhibiting a RAROC below 8% must be flagged for review and
remediation by the CRM department.
The RAROC floors are dynamic and subject to adjustment based on macroeconomic conditions, changes in the bank’s cost of
capital, or shifts in strategic priorities. The CPC conducts a comprehensive review of the thresholds annually, with interim
adjustments permitted in response to material changes in the risk environment. All adjustments must be documented and
communicated to relevant stakeholders."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

