<urn:cpi:source:15:98> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 98" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 3 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """3. 3. Governance
The governance of DSCR minimums under this clause is overseen by the Retail Credit Risk Committee (RCRC), which reports
directly to the Chief Risk Officer (CRO). The RCRC is responsible for approving any changes to the DSCR thresholds, ensuring
alignment with the bank's risk appetite, and monitoring compliance with this clause. The committee meets quarterly to review
the effectiveness of the DSCR policy and recommend adjustments as necessary.
The Underwriting Standards Team, a subcommittee of the RCRC, is tasked with the day-to-day implementation of this clause.
This team is responsible for developing underwriting guidelines, training materials, and tools to support the consistent
application of DSCR minimums. They also conduct periodic audits to verify adherence to the policy and address any identified
gaps or inconsistencies.
The Credit Policy Owner, a senior role within the Risk Management Division, is accountable for the overall integrity and
currency of this clause. This individual ensures that the policy remains aligned with industry best practices, regulatory
expectations, and the bank's strategic objectives. The Credit Policy Owner is also responsible for communicating policy updates
to all relevant stakeholders and ensuring that changes are implemented in a timely manner.
Compliance with this clause is monitored by the Internal Audit Department, which conducts independent reviews of the
underwriting and monitoring processes. Audit findings are reported to the RCRC and the bank's Board of Directors, with
corrective actions tracked to closure. The Internal Audit Department also validates that exceptions to the DSCR minimums are
properly documented, approved, and reported.
All employees involved in the retail credit process are required to complete annual training on this clause and the parent policy
CP-Retail-v3.1. Training records are maintained by the Learning and Development Department and are subject to review during
audits. Failure to comply with the governance requirements of this clause may result in disciplinary action, up to and including
termination of employment."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:53:329> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 329" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:53> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "15"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b80889a26cb577a996729bcf2be14b0134ee6688799b0a90edb704a5bd0b1958"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria outlined in this synthetic policy are designed to ensure that all CRE loans adhere to the bank’s risk
appetite and sub-sector limits. Underwriters must evaluate each loan application against a set of qualitative and quantitative
criteria, which include but are not limited to borrower creditworthiness, property cash flow, loan-to-value (LTV) ratios, debt
service coverage ratios (DSCR), and sub-sector exposure.
For borrower creditworthiness, underwriters must assess the financial strength, experience, and track record of the borrower or
sponsoring entity. This includes a review of financial statements, credit history, and prior project performance. Borrowers must
demonstrate the ability to service debt obligations under stress scenarios, with a minimum DSCR of 1.25x for most sub-sectors.
Higher DSCR thresholds may be required for higher-risk sub-sectors, such as hospitality, where a minimum of 1.40x may apply.
Property-level underwriting must include a thorough analysis of the property’s cash flow, occupancy rates, tenant quality, and
market position. For office and retail properties, underwriters must evaluate lease rollover risk, tenant concentration, and the
property’s ability to attract and retain tenants. Industrial properties require an assessment of location, accessibility, and demand
drivers, while multifamily properties must demonstrate stable occupancy and rental income.
LTV ratios are capped at 70% for most sub-sectors, with stricter limits of 60% for higher-risk sub-sectors such as hospitality or
specialized real estate. Exceptions to these limits require approval from the CRE Risk Management Team and must be justified
by compensating factors, such as strong borrower credit or exceptional property cash flow.
Underwriters are also responsible for ensuring that the loan complies with the sub-sector exposure limits defined in Section 5. If
a loan approval would cause the bank’s exposure to a sub-sector to exceed its limit, the underwriter must escalate the request
to the CRE Risk Management Team for further review and potential exception processing."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:72:1617> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1617" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:72> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "34"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "1dd66e05d6be0b8de2ae8d5020a930941c17fa515a794474c89f049c3098bae3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
This Policy establishes a series of quantitative limits to control the Bank’s exposure to duration risk. These limits are designed
to be dynamic, reflecting changes in market conditions, the Bank’s risk appetite, and regulatory requirements. The limits are
monitored on a daily basis by the Risk Management team, with breaches escalated immediately to the relevant business unit
and the RMC. Synthetic thresholds are provided below to illustrate the Policy’s approach, though actual limits must be
calibrated to the Bank’s specific risk profile.
The primary limit is the Portfolio Duration Limit, which caps the weighted average duration of the Bank’s credit portfolio at 4.5
years. This limit applies to the aggregate portfolio, with sub-limits established for individual business lines. For example, the
Corporate Banking portfolio may have a sub-limit of 5.0 years, while the Commercial Real Estate portfolio may be capped at 3.5
years due to its higher sensitivity to economic downturns. Business units must ensure that their portfolios remain within these
sub-limits at all times, with any breaches requiring immediate remediation.
In addition to portfolio-level limits, the Policy imposes transaction-level limits to prevent excessive concentration in long-duration
assets. For instance, no single credit exposure may have a duration exceeding 10 years without approval from the RMC.
Similarly, the Bank’s exposure to any single borrower with a duration of greater than 7 years may not exceed 5% of the Bank’s
Tier 1 capital. These limits are designed to prevent idiosyncratic risks from overwhelming the Bank’s risk management
capabilities.
The Policy also includes limits on the mismatch between the duration of the Bank’s assets and liabilities. The Asset-Liability
Duration Gap Limit restricts the difference between the weighted average duration of the Bank’s credit assets and its funding
liabilities to a maximum of 2.0 years. This limit is monitored by the Treasury function in conjunction with Risk Management, and
breaches must be addressed through liability management actions, such as issuing long-term debt or entering into interest rate
swaps.
Finally, the Policy establishes a series of early warning thresholds, which are set at 80% of the formal limits. For example, if the
Portfolio Duration Limit is 4.5 years, the early warning threshold would be 3.6 years. Exceeding an early warning threshold does
not constitute a breach but triggers a review by the relevant business unit and Risk Management to determine whether
corrective action is necessary. These thresholds are designed to provide advance notice of potential limit breaches and to
encourage proactive risk management."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:13:61> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 61" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
Clause 4.3 Tenor Limits establishes quantitative thresholds to guide the structuring of credit facilities and ensure consistency
across the bank’s wholesale portfolio. These limits are designed to balance the bank’s risk appetite with its commitment to
supporting client needs and are applied based on the borrower’s credit rating, industry, and the type of facility.
For term loans, the maximum tenor is 10 years for borrowers rated BBB- or higher by a recognized credit rating agency. For
borrowers rated below BBB-, the maximum tenor is 5 years. These limits are designed to reflect the higher probability of default
and the increased volatility in cash flows associated with lower-rated borrowers.
Revolving credit facilities are subject to a maximum tenor of 5 years, regardless of the borrower’s credit rating. This limit reflects
the short-term nature of these facilities and the need for regular reassessment of the borrower’s creditworthiness. Exceptions to
this limit may be granted for facilities that are fully secured by high-quality collateral, subject to approval by the GCC.
Trade finance facilities, including letters of credit and bank guarantees, are typically limited to a maximum tenor of 1 year.
However, for long-term trade finance transactions, such as those supporting infrastructure projects, the tenor may be extended
up to 5 years, provided that the underlying transaction is supported by robust cash flows and adequate collateral.
The bank also imposes aggregate tenor limits on its wholesale portfolio to prevent excessive concentration in long-term
exposures. For example, no more than 30% of the bank’s total wholesale credit portfolio may have a tenor exceeding 5 years.
This limit is monitored on a quarterly basis by CRM, and any breaches must be reported to the GCC for remediation."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1556> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1556" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Underwriting Criteria and Evidence Requirements
The underwriting criteria specified in RAT-1.0 are designed to ensure that credit decisions are based on a thorough and
rigorous analysis of all relevant factors. Underwriters must adhere to these criteria when evaluating new credit applications or
reviewing existing exposures. The criteria are structured to align with the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory expectations,
ensuring that only counterparties meeting the minimum standards are approved for credit facilities.
For corporate and commercial lending, underwriters are required to obtain and analyze a comprehensive set of financial
statements, including audited annual reports, interim financial statements, and management accounts. The financial statements
must cover at least the past three years, unless the counterparty is a startup or has a shorter operating history. Underwriters
must also review the counterparty’s business plan, cash flow projections, and any available market research reports.
In addition to financial data, underwriters must gather qualitative evidence to support their assessment. This includes
conducting site visits, interviewing key management personnel, and reviewing industry reports from reputable sources. For
counterparties operating in high-risk industries or jurisdictions, underwriters must also obtain independent third-party
assessments, such as due diligence reports or environmental, social, and governance (ESG) evaluations.
For retail lending, underwriters must verify the applicant’s identity, income, and employment status using primary sources, such
as government-issued identification, pay stubs, and employer verification letters. Credit reports from at least one major credit
bureau must be obtained and reviewed for all retail applicants. In cases where the applicant’s credit history is limited or
unavailable, underwriters may use alternative data sources, such as utility payment records or rental history, provided these are
documented and justified.
All evidence gathered during the underwriting process must be retained in the bank’s credit file system for a minimum of seven
years from the date of the last action on the credit facility. The evidence must be organized and labeled clearly to facilitate
future reviews or audits. Underwriters are responsible for ensuring that the credit file is complete and accurate before submitting
their recommendation to the appropriate approval authority."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:51:292> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 292" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:51> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "13"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "b699e0131a8b6811106a8f2e6b270c4c07dbdbfb4dcab01531aecf7f82001a12"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approval Authorities and Escalation Paths
Approval authorities for credit transactions are delegated based on the size, complexity, and RAROC compliance of the
exposure. For transactions meeting or exceeding the RAROC floors, approval authority is delegated to the Business Unit Head
for exposures up to USD 50 million. Exposures between USD 50 million and USD 200 million require approval from the
Regional Credit Committee (RCC), while those exceeding USD 200 million must be approved by the Global Credit Committee
(GCC).
Transactions that do not meet the RAROC floors are subject to an escalation process, beginning with the Business Unit Head,
who must provide a detailed justification for the deviation. If the Business Unit Head approves the exception, it must be
escalated to the Head of CRM for review. The Head of CRM has the authority to approve exceptions for exposures up to USD"""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:73:1637> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1637" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:73> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "35"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f55b2599f076c2c3cd110aa13a8fa88abde99e1a4028d824284dba40e3093b68"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
This Policy establishes a series of quantitative limits to control liquidity risk within the credit portfolio. These limits are designed
to ensure that the Bank maintains sufficient liquid assets to meet its obligations and absorb potential liquidity shocks. The
primary liquidity metric under this Policy is the Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR), which measures the Bank’s high-quality liquid
assets (HQLA) against its net cash outflows over a 30-day stress period. The Bank’s target LCR for the credit portfolio is 120%,
with a minimum threshold of 100%.
In addition to the LCR, the Policy sets a Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR) target of 110% for the credit portfolio. The NSFR
measures the amount of stable funding available to the Bank relative to the liquidity profiles of its assets and off-balance-sheet
exposures over a one-year horizon. The NSFR ensures that the Bank’s long-term funding profile is aligned with the liquidity
characteristics of its credit assets, reducing the risk of funding mismatches.
The Policy also imposes concentration limits on illiquid credit assets. No more than 20% of the Bank’s total credit portfolio may
consist of illiquid assets, defined as those that cannot be converted into cash within 30 days without a significant loss of value.
Within this 20% limit, no single illiquid asset or group of related illiquid assets may exceed 5% of the total credit portfolio. These
limits are monitored daily by CRM and reported to ALCO on a monthly basis.
Liquidity buffers are maintained to cover potential cash flow disruptions from the credit portfolio. The Bank holds a dedicated
liquidity buffer equal to 10% of its total credit exposures, composed of HQLA as defined by regulatory standards. This buffer is
separate from the Bank’s general liquidity reserves and is earmarked specifically for credit portfolio liquidity needs. The
adequacy of the buffer is reviewed quarterly by ALCO, with adjustments made based on changes in the credit portfolio’s risk
profile.
The Policy includes early warning triggers for liquidity risk. If the LCR for the credit portfolio falls below 110%, or if the
concentration of illiquid assets exceeds 15%, CRM must immediately notify ALCO and the CRO. In such cases, a liquidity
action plan must be developed and implemented within 10 business days to restore compliance with the Policy’s thresholds.
Failure to address breaches within this timeframe may result in restrictions on new credit originations or other remedial actions."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:version:47> a cpi:PolicyVersion ;
    rdfs:label "4.2 Country Risk v1.0" ;
    cpi:APPLIES_IN <urn:cpi:geography:72f98d44d5fd2b202514> ;
    cpi:CONTAINS_SECTION <urn:cpi:section:3fbace7a347d1316e1af32be>,
        <urn:cpi:section:53bc25ba13edc23c1b5226bf> ;
