    cpi:HAS_EFFECTIVE_PERIOD <urn:cpi:version:47:effective-period> ;
    cpi:HAS_EXCLUSION <urn:cpi:version:47:scope:logic:fe6f749cc4500851> ;
    cpi:VERSION_OF <urn:cpi:policy:9> ;
    cpi:sourceRef <urn:cpi:source:47:201> ;
    cpi:source_checksum "94dc4af6acabbacb87abaf31760cbf7b02d3c70b267457e9f40c7c06d0459c5a"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:58:432> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 432" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:58> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "20"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "124566926bfd9f4ebe2d18795df04f73f70fba22a895c4181d767a9bf0156aaa"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
The Capital Floors Policy establishes quantitative limits to ensure that the Bank’s capital adequacy ratio remains above the
minimum regulatory requirements. The primary limit is the capital floor, which is set at 72.5% of the RWAs calculated using the
standardized approach. This means that the RWAs calculated under the Bank’s internal models must not be less than 72.5% of
the RWAs calculated under the standardized approach for the same exposures.
In addition to the capital floor, the Policy introduces a series of internal triggers and thresholds to monitor and manage capital
adequacy. The first trigger is activated when the Bank’s Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1) capital ratio falls below 10.5%. At this
point, the RMD must conduct a detailed review of the Bank’s capital position and submit a report to the RMC within 10 business
days, outlining the causes of the decline and the proposed corrective actions.
A second trigger is activated when the CET1 capital ratio falls below 9.5%. In this case, the RMC must convene an emergency
meeting within 5 business days to assess the situation and develop a capital restoration plan. The plan must include specific
measures to improve the capital ratio, such as reducing risk-weighted assets, raising additional capital, or divesting non-core
assets. The plan must be approved by the Board and submitted to the relevant regulators within 15 business days.
The Policy also establishes a maximum leverage ratio of 5%, calculated as the ratio of the Bank’s Tier 1 capital to its total
exposure measure. The total exposure measure includes both on-balance sheet and off-balance sheet exposures. If the
leverage ratio exceeds 5%, the Bank must immediately notify the RMC and implement measures to reduce the ratio to within
the limit.
For trading book exposures, the Policy sets a limit on the Value-at-Risk (VaR) capital charge. The VaR capital charge must not
exceed 3 times the average VaR capital charge over the previous 60 business days. If this limit is breached, the Bank must
conduct a review of its trading activities and risk management practices and report the findings to the RMC."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "Basel IV"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:73:1647> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1647" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:73> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "35"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 10 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f55b2599f076c2c3cd110aa13a8fa88abde99e1a4028d824284dba40e3093b68"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """10. 10. Policy Review and Maintenance
This Policy is subject to regular review to ensure that it remains effective, relevant, and aligned with the Bank’s evolving risk
profile, regulatory requirements, and industry best practices. The review process is designed to identify gaps, address emerging
risks, and incorporate lessons learned from internal and external events. The Policy is a living document that evolves in
response to changes in the Bank’s business, the economic environment, and the regulatory landscape.
The CRO is responsible for initiating and overseeing the review of this Policy at least annually. The review process involves a
comprehensive assessment of the Policy’s provisions, including its governance framework, underwriting criteria, quantitative
limits, and reporting requirements. Input is sought from key stakeholders, including ALCO, CRM, Treasury, business units, and
Internal Audit, to ensure that the Policy reflects the needs and perspectives of all relevant parties.
In addition to the annual review, the Policy may be updated more frequently in response to material changes in the Bank’s
credit portfolio, liquidity risk appetite, or regulatory expectations. For example, if the Bank enters a new market or product line
with unique liquidity characteristics, the Policy may need to be amended to address the associated risks. Similarly, if regulatory
authorities issue new liquidity risk management guidelines, the Policy will be updated to ensure compliance.
Amendments to this Policy must be approved by ALCO and, if material, by the Board’s Risk Committee. Material amendments
include changes to quantitative limits, governance structures, or approval authorities, as well as the addition or removal of key
controls. Non-material amendments, such as clarifications or minor updates to reporting requirements, may be approved by the
CRO in consultation with the CCO. All amendments are documented in the Policy’s revision history, which is maintained by
CRM.
The effectiveness of this Policy is also evaluated through periodic self-assessments and independent reviews. CRM conducts a
self-assessment of the Policy’s implementation at least annually, focusing on the adequacy of controls, the accuracy of
reporting, and the compliance of business units with the Policy’s provisions. Internal Audit conducts an independent review of
the Policy at least every two years, with the findings reported to the Audit Committee of the Board. The results of these
assessments are used to inform the Policy’s review and update process."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1547> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1547" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 1 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """1. 1. Purpose and Applicability
This document, titled RAT-1.0 Credit Rating Standards, establishes the foundational framework for credit rating assignments
within the synthetic demonstration environment of the bank. It is designed to ensure consistency, transparency, and regulatory
compliance in the evaluation of creditworthiness for all counterparties, including corporate, commercial, and retail clients. The
standards outlined herein are aligned with the parent policy RAT-v1.0 and serve as a critical component of the bank’s risk
management infrastructure.
The primary purpose of RAT-1.0 is to define the methodologies, criteria, and governance structures required to assign, validate,
and monitor credit ratings. These ratings are integral to the bank’s decision-making processes, including loan approvals,
risk-based pricing, capital allocation, and portfolio monitoring. By adhering to these standards, the bank aims to mitigate credit
risk, enhance stakeholder confidence, and fulfill its fiduciary responsibilities.
This policy applies to all business units, risk management teams, and personnel involved in the credit assessment process. It is
mandatory for underwriters, credit analysts, relationship managers, and senior management to comply with the provisions of
this document. Third-party vendors or partners engaged in credit-related activities on behalf of the bank must also adhere to
these standards unless explicitly exempted by the Chief Risk Officer (CRO).
The synthetic nature of this document is emphasized to clarify that it is intended for demonstration, training, and internal testing
purposes only. While the thresholds, roles, and processes described are realistic and internally consistent, they do not reflect
actual bank operations or real-world data. Any use of this document for live transactions or external reporting must be explicitly
authorized by the bank’s compliance and legal departments.
The applicability of RAT-1.0 extends to all credit exposures, including but not limited to loans, bonds, derivatives, and trade
finance instruments. It covers both new credit applications and the periodic review of existing exposures. The standards are
designed to be scalable, accommodating the diverse needs of the bank’s global operations while maintaining uniformity in risk
assessment practices."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:54:353> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 353" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:54> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "16"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "350cf7f7d62e85428cf6a9698bfd3ab07d8e9c57b05722882ba5e0af64e6dcab"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approval Authorities and Escalation Paths
Approval authorities for construction loans are tiered based on the loan amount, risk profile, and complexity of the project.
Relationship Managers may approve construction loans up to $5 million, provided the loan meets all underwriting criteria and
the borrower has an existing relationship with the bank. Loans between $5 million and $10 million require approval from the
Regional Credit Officer (RCO) in addition to the Relationship Manager.
Loans exceeding $10 million must be approved by the Construction Lending Committee (CLC). The CLC’s approval is also
required for any loan that involves speculative development, a borrower with a credit rating below BBB-, or a project located in a
market where the bank has limited experience. The CLC’s decision is final, unless overridden by the Chief Credit Officer (CCO)
in exceptional circumstances.
Escalation paths are clearly defined to ensure timely resolution of issues. Any deviations from underwriting criteria, such as LTC
or LTV ratios exceeding policy limits, must be escalated to the RCO for review. The RCO has the authority to approve
exceptions up to 5% above the policy limits, provided the deviation is justified by compensating factors such as strong borrower
financials or pre-leasing commitments.
Material exceptions, defined as deviations exceeding 5% of policy limits or involving high-risk features such as speculative
development in an untried market, must be escalated to the CLC. The CLC will review the exception request, along with any
mitigating factors, and provide a written decision within 10 business days. All exception requests and decisions are documented
in the bank’s exception tracking system and reported to the CCO on a quarterly basis.
In cases where a construction loan exhibits signs of distress, such as cost overruns exceeding 10% of the budget or delays in
construction milestones, the Relationship Manager must immediately escalate the matter to the Construction Risk Officer
(CRO). The CRO will assess the situation and determine whether to place the loan on the watchlist, require additional collateral,
or take other risk mitigation actions. The CRO’s findings and recommendations are presented to the CLC for further action."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:49:258> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 258" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:49> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "11"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 9 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "067ea0df55a1950a6f414e2436d7ac36cad81a25ad50f752f9ba8449ed70a5eb"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """9. 9. Reporting
Reporting is a key control mechanism for ensuring transparency and accountability in the management of eligible collateral. The
bank maintains a comprehensive reporting framework that provides timely and accurate information to stakeholders at all
levels, from front-line credit officers to the Board of Directors. Reports are designed to support decision-making, monitor
compliance with the policy, and identify emerging risks.
The CMU is responsible for generating and distributing a suite of standard reports, which are produced at varying frequencies
depending on the audience and the nature of the information. Daily reports are generated for liquid collateral, such as cash and
securities, to track market values and margin requirements. Weekly reports are produced for less liquid assets, such as real
estate and inventory, to monitor valuation adjustments and compliance with LTV limits.
Monthly reports are provided to the CRC and CAC, summarizing the overall status of the collateral portfolio. These reports
include key metrics such as the total value of collateral, the distribution of collateral by asset class and region, and the number
and value of exceptions. They also highlight any breaches of policy limits, trends in collateral values, and the results of stress
testing. The reports are accompanied by a commentary from the CMU, which interprets the data and identifies any areas of
concern.
Quarterly reports are prepared for the Board of Directors, providing a high-level overview of the collateral portfolio’s
performance and its alignment with the bank’s risk appetite. These reports include a summary of the key risks and issues
identified during the quarter, as well as the actions taken to address them. They also provide an update on any changes to the
policy or the regulatory environment that may impact the bank’s collateral management practices.
Ad-hoc reports are generated as needed to address specific requests from stakeholders or to respond to emerging risks. For
example, if a significant market event occurs, such as a sharp decline in real estate prices, the CMU may produce a special
report analyzing the potential impact on the bank’s collateral portfolio. All reports are archived for audit purposes and are
subject to review by Internal Audit to ensure their accuracy and completeness."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:69:1549> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 1549" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:69> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "31"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "e6bb9031cfd51e60c492e3ee404912c4ccff3400a083ae4d6675fdf73e31d0b9"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope and Exclusions
The scope of RAT-1.0 Credit Rating Standards encompasses all credit rating activities conducted by the bank, including the
initial assignment, periodic review, and ad-hoc reassessment of ratings. This includes ratings for internal risk management
purposes as well as those used for regulatory reporting, such as under the Basel III framework. The policy is global in nature,
applying to all jurisdictions in which the bank operates, unless local regulations impose stricter requirements.
All credit exposures, regardless of size or complexity, fall within the scope of this policy. This includes exposures to corporate
entities, small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), retail customers, financial institutions, and sovereign entities. The policy
also covers specialized lending areas such as project finance, leveraged finance, and structured credit products. Each
exposure must be assigned a credit rating in accordance with the methodologies outlined in this document.
Exclusions from this policy are limited and must be explicitly documented and approved by the CRO. Typical exclusions may
include intra-group exposures, where the counterparty is a subsidiary or affiliate of the bank, and exposures that are fully
guaranteed by a sovereign entity with a risk weight of 0% under regulatory capital rules. Additionally, exposures that are
immaterial in size (defined as less than USD 100,000) may be exempted from formal rating assignments, provided they are
subject to alternative risk controls.
The policy also excludes credit ratings assigned by external credit rating agencies (e.g., Moody’s, S&P, Fitch) for the purpose of
regulatory compliance, where such ratings are used directly without internal override. However, the bank reserves the right to
perform its own internal rating assessment for risk management purposes, even when external ratings are available. In such
cases, the internal rating must be documented and justified in accordance with the standards set forth in this policy.
Any deviations from the scope of this policy, including temporary or permanent exclusions, must be documented in the bank’s
Risk Policy Exception Log. Such deviations require the approval of the CRO and must be reviewed at least annually to ensure
they remain appropriate. The bank’s internal audit function will periodically verify compliance with the scope and exclusion
provisions of this policy."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:13:59> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 59" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria for tenor limits are designed to ensure that the tenor of any credit facility is commensurate with the
borrower’s creditworthiness, the purpose of the facility, and the bank’s risk appetite. The primary factors considered in
determining the appropriate tenor include the borrower’s financial strength, cash flow stability, industry risk, and the economic
environment.
For investment-grade corporates, the maximum tenor for term loans is set at 10 years, provided that the borrower demonstrates
strong and stable cash flows, a low leverage ratio, and a history of prudent financial management. For non-investment-grade
corporates, the maximum tenor is limited to 5 years, reflecting the higher risk of default and the need for more frequent credit
reviews.
The purpose of the credit facility also plays a critical role in determining the tenor. For example, working capital facilities typically
have shorter tenors, often not exceeding 3 years, as they are intended to support short-term liquidity needs. In contrast, project
finance facilities may have longer tenors, up to 15 years, to align with the long-term nature of the underlying assets and cash
flows.
Underwriters are required to conduct a thorough analysis of the borrower’s ability to service debt over the proposed tenor. This
analysis includes stress testing the borrower’s financials under various scenarios, such as economic downturns,
industry-specific shocks, and changes in interest rates. The results of these stress tests must be documented in the credit
proposal and reviewed by CRM.
Collateral and other credit enhancements may influence the tenor limits. For instance, facilities that are fully secured by
high-quality collateral may qualify for longer tenors, as the bank’s risk is mitigated by the presence of the collateral. However,
the tenor must still be consistent with the bank’s overall risk appetite and the borrower’s ability to generate sufficient cash flows
to service the debt."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

