<urn:cpi:source:1:10> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 10" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:1> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "21b6dc2ac5a7b626d6f486564ebfa1ffd0d49d8a47aec79f488f82a87ccfcb8e"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
Quantitative limits are established to ensure that credit exposures remain within the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory
constraints. These limits are applied at various levels, including individual borrowers, industry sectors, geographic regions, and
product types. The limits are designed to prevent excessive concentration risk and to ensure diversification across the
wholesale portfolio.
The bank’s aggregate wholesale credit exposure to a single borrower or borrower group must not exceed 15% of the bank’s
Tier 1 capital, as defined by regulatory guidelines. For Tier 1 clients, the maximum exposure is capped at USD 500 million,
unless approved by the GCPC. For Tier 2 clients, the cap is USD 200 million, and for Tier 3 clients, it is USD 50 million.
Exposures exceeding these thresholds require a detailed concentration risk analysis and approval from the CCO for Wholesale
Banking.
Industry sector limits are set to prevent over-exposure to any single industry. The bank’s aggregate exposure to a single
industry sector must not exceed 25% of the total wholesale portfolio. Industries with higher risk ratings, such as High or
Moderate-High, are subject to lower limits. For example, the aggregate exposure to the commodities trading sector is capped at
10% of the wholesale portfolio. Sector limits are monitored monthly, and breaches must be reported to the GCPC within five
business days.
Geographic limits are applied to manage country risk and ensure diversification across regions. The bank’s aggregate exposure
to a single country must not exceed 20% of the total wholesale portfolio. For countries with a sovereign credit rating below
BBB-, the limit is reduced to 10%. Exposures to countries subject to international sanctions or with significant political instability
are prohibited unless explicitly approved by the GCPC.
Product-type limits are established to manage the risk associated with different credit products. For example, the aggregate
exposure to derivative transactions must not exceed 15% of the wholesale portfolio. Similarly, the exposure to unsecured credit
facilities is capped at 30% of the portfolio. These limits are designed to ensure that the bank’s risk profile remains balanced and
that no single product type dominates the portfolio."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:15:102> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 102" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
The quantitative limits for DSCR minimums are structured to align with the bank's risk appetite and the specific characteristics
of retail credit products. For standard retail loans, the minimum DSCR threshold is 1.25x. This threshold is applied to all
borrowers unless they qualify for a tiered adjustment based on their credit score or other risk factors, as outlined in Section 4.
For revolving credit products, such as credit cards and home equity lines of credit, the DSCR minimum is set at 1.10x. This
lower threshold reflects the flexible nature of revolving credit, where borrowers may not utilize the full credit line at all times.
However, underwriters must still verify that the borrower's cash flow is sufficient to service the maximum potential debt under
the revolving facility.
For secured retail loans, such as auto loans or secured personal loans, the DSCR minimum is 1.20x. The collateral securing
these loans provides an additional layer of risk mitigation, allowing for a slightly lower DSCR threshold. However, underwriters
must ensure that the value of the collateral is sufficient to cover the outstanding debt in the event of default.
The bank imposes a hard floor DSCR minimum of 1.00x for all retail credit products. No borrower may be approved with a
DSCR below this threshold, regardless of compensating factors or exceptions. This hard floor is a non-negotiable control
designed to prevent excessive leverage and protect the bank's portfolio quality.
DSCR thresholds are reviewed annually by the RCRC to ensure they remain appropriate given the economic environment,
portfolio performance, and regulatory expectations. Adjustments to the thresholds must be approved by the CRO and
communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The bank reserves the right to temporarily tighten or relax DSCR thresholds in
response to significant economic or market events."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:version:37> a cpi:PolicyVersion ;
    rdfs:label "3.5 Sector Concentration v1.0" ;
    cpi:CONTAINS_SECTION <urn:cpi:section:0b69d1495d4fe585f3852657>,
        <urn:cpi:section:4525e8e32b9afaf319fc5125>,
        <urn:cpi:section:58894c67e1b48af03e39bc60>,
        <urn:cpi:section:6a01a2dcc2d1350850dd225a>,
        <urn:cpi:section:857ac18c6693c7975527e660>,
        <urn:cpi:section:f14135b338cc9d0501e4e0b0> ;
    cpi:HAS_EFFECTIVE_PERIOD <urn:cpi:version:37:effective-period> ;
    cpi:VERSION_OF <urn:cpi:policy:8> ;
    cpi:sourceRef <urn:cpi:source:37:172> ;
    cpi:source_checksum "d2ec1d78bee541d14bace4fea235eed9fcf460eb088763e820659985702df05c"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:version "1.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:15:100> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 100" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:15> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "58065e833dd68879f239c9bc9b41406402af03091875effc1641a6145f3bc489"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria for DSCR minimums are designed to ensure that borrowers demonstrate sufficient cash flow to service
their debt obligations. For retail credit products, the DSCR is calculated as the ratio of the borrower's net operating income to
their total debt service, including principal, interest, and any other mandatory payments. The minimum DSCR threshold for
standard retail loans is set at 1.25x, meaning that the borrower's net operating income must be at least 1.25 times their total
debt service.
For borrowers with a credit score below 650, the minimum DSCR threshold is increased to 1.50x to account for the higher risk
profile. Conversely, borrowers with a credit score above 750 and a demonstrated history of stable income may qualify for a
reduced DSCR minimum of 1.15x, subject to additional controls and approvals. These thresholds are synthetic and designed to
illustrate a tiered approach to risk-based underwriting.
Underwriters are required to use the bank's approved financial analysis tools to calculate DSCR. These tools automatically pull
income and debt data from the borrower's application, credit report, and other verified sources. Manual calculations are
permitted only in exceptional circumstances and must be documented and approved by a senior underwriter or credit manager.
In addition to the DSCR calculation, underwriters must consider the borrower's debt-to-income (DTI) ratio, employment stability,
and other qualitative factors. A borrower may meet the DSCR minimum but still be deemed ineligible for credit if other risk
factors are present. Conversely, a borrower who falls slightly below the DSCR minimum may be approved with compensating
factors, such as a high credit score, significant liquid assets, or a strong repayment history.
All underwriting decisions must be documented in the bank's loan origination system, including the DSCR calculation, the
threshold applied, and any compensating factors considered. This documentation is subject to review by the Underwriting
Standards Team and Internal Audit to ensure compliance with this clause and the parent policy CP-Retail-v3.1."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:1:12> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 12" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:1> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 6 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "21b6dc2ac5a7b626d6f486564ebfa1ffd0d49d8a47aec79f488f82a87ccfcb8e"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """6. 6. Approval Authorities and Escalation Paths
Approval authorities are delegated based on the risk profile, size, and complexity of the credit exposure. The approval matrix
ensures that higher-risk or larger exposures receive appropriate levels of scrutiny and oversight. The matrix is structured to
align with the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory expectations, and it is reviewed annually by the GCPC.
For Tier 3 clients with exposures up to USD 10 million, approval authority is delegated to the regional credit officer. Exposures
between USD 10 million and USD 50 million require approval from the regional credit committee, which includes
representatives from the Wholesale Credit Risk Division and the business line. For Tier 2 clients, exposures up to USD 50
million require regional credit committee approval, while exposures between USD 50 million and USD 200 million require
approval from the global credit committee.
Tier 1 clients and exposures exceeding USD 200 million require approval from the GCPC. The GCPC meets monthly to review
large or complex credit requests and ensures that all material risks are identified and mitigated. In urgent cases, the CCO for
Wholesale Banking may grant interim approval, but this must be ratified by the GCPC at its next scheduled meeting.
Escalation paths are clearly defined to ensure that any credit request that does not meet the underwriting standards or exceeds
delegated authorities is promptly escalated to the appropriate level. For example, if a credit analyst identifies a material
weakness in a borrower’s financial position that was not addressed in the initial application, the request must be escalated to
the regional credit officer for further review. Similarly, if a relationship manager proposes a structure that deviates from the
bank’s standard terms, the request must be escalated to the global credit committee.
All approvals and escalations must be documented in the credit file, including the rationale for the decision, any conditions or
covenants imposed, and the names of the approving authorities. The credit file must also include evidence of compliance with
the underwriting standards, such as financial statements, industry analyses, and collateral valuations. Failure to obtain the
required approvals or to document the decision-making process may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination
of employment."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:1:4> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 4" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:1> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "1"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "21b6dc2ac5a7b626d6f486564ebfa1ffd0d49d8a47aec79f488f82a87ccfcb8e"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope
The scope of this clause, 4.1 Underwriting Standards, encompasses all wholesale credit exposures originated, renewed, or
amended by the bank. Wholesale credit exposures are defined as those extended to corporate entities, financial institutions,
sovereigns, and other non-retail counterparties with an aggregate exposure of USD 1,000,000 or more. This threshold ensures
that the underwriting standards are applied consistently across the bank’s wholesale portfolio.
The clause applies to all business lines and geographic regions where the bank operates, unless explicitly exempted by the
Global Credit Policy Committee. Exemptions must be documented and justified based on local regulatory requirements or
unique market conditions. All personnel involved in the underwriting process, including relationship managers, credit analysts,
and approval authorities, are required to comply with the standards outlined in this clause.
The underwriting standards cover the entire lifecycle of a credit exposure, from initial due diligence and approval to ongoing
monitoring and renewal. This includes the evaluation of new credit requests, amendments to existing facilities, and the annual
or event-driven reviews of creditworthiness. The standards are also applicable to the underwriting of derivative transactions,
trade finance, and other non-traditional credit products offered to wholesale clients.
For the purposes of this clause, wholesale clients are categorized into three tiers based on their annual revenue: Tier 1 (USD 1
billion and above), Tier 2 (USD 250 million to USD 999 million), and Tier 3 (USD 1 million to USD 249 million). The underwriting
requirements, including the depth of analysis and approval thresholds, are tailored to the risk profile of each tier. Higher-risk
industries, such as commodities trading or highly leveraged sectors, may be subject to additional underwriting scrutiny.
This clause does not apply to retail or small business lending, which are governed by separate credit policies. However, it is
acknowledged that certain wholesale clients may have retail or small business subsidiaries. In such cases, the underwriting of
exposures to these subsidiaries must be evaluated in the context of the parent company’s overall creditworthiness and the
bank’s aggregate exposure to the group."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:49:244> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 244" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:49> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "11"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "067ea0df55a1950a6f414e2436d7ac36cad81a25ad50f752f9ba8449ed70a5eb"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope
The scope of this clause, 2.2 Eligible Collateral, encompasses all credit facilities extended by the bank where collateral is
required or provided as security. This includes but is not limited to commercial loans, retail mortgages, asset-backed lending,
and trade finance transactions. The policy applies globally across all business units, subsidiaries, and jurisdictions in which the
bank operates, unless local regulations dictate otherwise.
Collateral eligible under this policy includes tangible assets such as real estate, machinery, inventory, and financial instruments
like bonds, equities, and cash deposits. Intangible assets, such as intellectual property or accounts receivable, may also be
considered, provided they meet the bank’s eligibility criteria and can be reliably valued and liquidated. The policy explicitly
excludes assets that are illiquid, difficult to value, or subject to significant legal or regulatory restrictions.
The policy is binding on all employees, contractors, and third-party service providers involved in the origination, underwriting,
monitoring, or enforcement of credit facilities. Compliance with this clause is mandatory, and any deviations must be
documented, justified, and approved through the bank’s exception management process. The synthetic nature of this data
ensures that it aligns with demonstration objectives while maintaining internal consistency.
Geographically, the policy applies to all regions where the bank operates, though local adaptations may be necessary to comply
with jurisdiction-specific laws or regulatory requirements. For example, certain jurisdictions may impose restrictions on the types
of collateral that can be pledged or the methods of valuation. In such cases, local credit policies must align with this global
framework while addressing regional nuances.
The scope also includes the bank’s internal systems and processes for tracking, valuing, and reporting on collateral. This
ensures that collateral management is integrated into the broader credit risk framework, providing a holistic view of the bank’s
secured exposures. The policy is designed to be scalable, accommodating the needs of both small and large credit facilities."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:55:369> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 369" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:55> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "17"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 4 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "38a6e70887fe9524760b8bed0f04a977db2eaac85d866baa58606dfeb258a05e"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """4. 4. Underwriting Criteria
The underwriting criteria outlined in this synthetic policy are designed to ensure that credit decisions are based on a thorough
assessment of the borrower’s financial health, repayment capacity, and collateral quality. The bank employs a risk-based
approach to underwriting, where the depth and rigor of the analysis are proportional to the size and complexity of the credit
exposure.
For corporate and commercial lending, the underwriting process includes a detailed review of the borrower’s financial
statements, cash flow projections, industry analysis, and management quality. The bank requires a minimum debt service
coverage ratio (DSCR) of 1.25x for term loans and a loan-to-value (LTV) ratio not exceeding 70% for secured transactions.
These thresholds are synthetic and intended for demonstration purposes.
Retail lending underwriting criteria include an assessment of the borrower’s credit score, income stability, and debt-to-income
(DTI) ratio. The bank sets a minimum credit score of 650 for unsecured personal loans and a maximum DTI ratio of 40% for
mortgage applications. These criteria are applied consistently across all retail lending products to ensure fairness and risk
mitigation.
The underwriting criteria are reviewed annually by the Credit Policy Team to ensure they remain aligned with the bank’s risk
appetite and market conditions. Any exceptions to the underwriting criteria must be justified, documented, and approved by the
appropriate authority as defined in the DoA matrix."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:version:50> a cpi:PolicyVersion ;
    rdfs:label "2.3 Haircuts v2.4" ;
    cpi:CONTAINS_SECTION <urn:cpi:section:1b45e2afe46891994d99e625>,
        <urn:cpi:section:4f1507d5b87c686e50563262>,
        <urn:cpi:section:6516fcd4347307c9ea10e99e>,
        <urn:cpi:section:7e81a572cde12383c40facb9>,
        <urn:cpi:section:7fe4271b12ffbc5a4f593db9>,
        <urn:cpi:section:8b90da604a661e626aa1ba5f>,
        <urn:cpi:section:b642db34d5bd084d7421ca81>,
        <urn:cpi:section:d2099055ffdb129e68a35ad1> ;
    cpi:HAS_EFFECTIVE_PERIOD <urn:cpi:version:50:effective-period> ;
    cpi:VERSION_OF <urn:cpi:policy:12> ;
    cpi:sourceRef <urn:cpi:source:50:261> ;
    cpi:source_checksum "57daf82286a273df900c1ea4ba94e1cc2f901b066d3e1221937e96760ef9739d"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:14:82> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 82" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:14> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "4"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "ed2c3ab4351c2b264e5cc27e6c013de3d4d8cee114f40e2c33328172889e1df5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2.5x for non-investment-grade borrowers. The Bank may impose lower thresholds for borrowers in high-risk industries or with
volatile cash flows. The leverage ratio must be tested quarterly, and the borrower must provide evidence of compliance in the
form of audited or reviewed financial statements.
The minimum Interest Coverage Ratio (EBITDA/Interest Expense) is set at 3.0x for investment-grade borrowers and 4.0x for
non-investment-grade borrowers. This covenant must be tested semi-annually, and the borrower must provide interim financial
statements to demonstrate compliance. The Bank may require higher thresholds for borrowers with significant
off-balance-sheet obligations or contingent liabilities.
The minimum Debt Service Coverage Ratio (Net Operating Income/Total Debt Service) is 1.25x for all borrowers. This covenant
must be tested annually, and the borrower must provide a cash flow statement prepared in accordance with the Bank’s
specified accounting standards. The Bank may require a higher threshold for borrowers with irregular cash flows or seasonal
revenue patterns.
For non-financial covenants, the Bank must establish clear and measurable thresholds where possible. For example, the
borrower may be required to maintain a minimum liquidity ratio of 1.0x (Current Assets/Current Liabilities) at all times. The Bank
must also define the consequences of a breach, including the requirement for immediate notification, the submission of a
corrective action plan, and potential acceleration of the loan."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:52:305> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 305" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:52> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "14"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "a31614bc9583cac5c205cf3ac248b30964fb2ecb3c011a9d90d81ca1e5d5d963"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope and Applicability
The Spread Grid under Clause 1.2 of Parent Policy PRC-v1.7 applies to all credit exposures originated, renewed, or amended
by the bank, including but not limited to corporate loans, commercial real estate financing, project finance, and fixed-income
securities. It covers both new transactions and the repricing of existing exposures, ensuring that all credit decisions are aligned
with the bank’s risk-based pricing framework.
Geographically, this policy is applicable to all jurisdictions in which the bank operates, unless local regulations or market
practices explicitly conflict with its provisions. In such cases, local credit policies must be harmonized with this Spread Grid to
the greatest extent possible, and any deviations must be documented and approved by the Group Risk Committee. This
synthetic document is designed to demonstrate how such harmonization might be structured in practice.
The Spread Grid is mandatory for all business units involved in credit origination, including Commercial Banking, Investment
Banking, and Treasury. It also applies to risk management functions responsible for monitoring portfolio spreads and ensuring
compliance with the policy. Third-party vendors or partners involved in credit transactions on behalf of the bank must adhere to
equivalent standards or provide evidence of comparable risk-based pricing frameworks.
Exposures exempt from the Spread Grid include interbank placements, sovereign debt, and transactions explicitly covered by
separate, specialized pricing policies (e.g., derivatives or structured products). However, even for exempt exposures, the
underlying principles of risk-based pricing and spread determination outlined in this document should be considered where
applicable. Exemptions must be clearly documented and justified in the credit approval memorandum.
The policy is effective immediately upon issuance and supersedes all prior versions of the Spread Grid. All existing credit
exposures must be reviewed against this policy within 180 days of its effective date to ensure compliance. Any gaps identified
during this review must be addressed through repricing, additional collateral, or other risk mitigation measures, as approved by
the relevant credit authority."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "1.7"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:54:351> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 351" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:54> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "16"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "350cf7f7d62e85428cf6a9698bfd3ab07d8e9c57b05722882ba5e0af64e6dcab"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
This policy establishes quantitative limits to control the bank’s exposure to construction lending risks. The maximum exposure
for any single construction loan is $50 million, unless approved by the CLC and the CCO. Aggregate exposure to construction
lending shall not exceed 25% of the bank’s total loan portfolio at any time. Additionally, the bank’s exposure to any single
borrower or borrower group for construction purposes is limited to 15% of the bank’s Tier 1 capital.
For speculative construction projects, the bank imposes a sub-limit of $25 million per loan and 10% of the total loan portfolio.
Speculative projects are defined as those where less than 50% of the units or space are pre-sold or pre-leased at the time of
loan closing. Projects exceeding these thresholds require additional approvals, including a unanimous vote by the CLC and
written justification from the Relationship Manager.
The bank maintains a contingency reserve requirement of 10% of the total project cost for all construction loans. This reserve is
held in a separate escrow account and released only upon completion of the project or as approved by the Construction Risk
Officer. In the event of cost overruns, the borrower is required to inject additional equity to cover the shortfall before any reserve
funds are released.
Interest rate risk is managed through the use of fixed-rate loans or interest rate caps for variable-rate construction loans. The
maximum allowable interest rate for construction loans is the bank’s prime rate plus 200 basis points. For loans with variable
rates, the bank requires a minimum debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) of 1.25x, calculated using a stressed interest rate of
prime + 300 basis points.
The bank also imposes limits on the duration of construction loans. The maximum term for a construction loan is 36 months,
with an option to extend for an additional 12 months subject to CLC approval. Loans that exceed 24 months in duration require
quarterly progress inspections by an independent third-party engineer, at the borrower’s expense. Failure to meet construction
milestones may result in the suspension of disbursements or acceleration of the loan."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.0"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:13:55> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 55" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:13> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "3"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 2 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "7af9f2abcbbb8744948914bbc8041bd5e8268bc55c583ef18af14a36ae656ad2"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """2. 2. Scope
Clause 4.3 Tenor Limits applies to all wholesale banking activities conducted by the bank, including its domestic and
international operations. The scope of this clause encompasses all credit facilities extended to corporate clients, financial
institutions, and other wholesale counterparties, regardless of the jurisdiction or currency of the transaction.
The policy is binding on all business units, including Corporate Banking, Investment Banking, Trade Finance, and Treasury, as
well as support functions such as Risk Management, Compliance, and Internal Audit. Each unit is responsible for ensuring that
its activities comply with the tenor limits and procedures outlined in this clause.
Exclusions from this policy include retail banking products, consumer lending, and credit card facilities, which are governed by
separate policies tailored to their unique risk profiles. Additionally, intra-group transactions and exposures to sovereign entities
may be subject to customized tenor limits as approved by the Group Risk Committee.
The synthetic demonstration data within this clause includes hypothetical tenor thresholds, such as a maximum tenor of 10
years for term loans to investment-grade corporates and a maximum tenor of 5 years for non-investment-grade corporates.
These thresholds are illustrative and designed to demonstrate how tenor limits might be structured in a real-world scenario.
All exceptions to the scope of this policy must be documented and approved by the Chief Risk Officer (CRO) or their designated
delegate. Such exceptions will be subject to enhanced monitoring and reporting to ensure that any deviations from the standard
tenor limits do not introduce unacceptable levels of risk to the bank."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "4.2"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:56:392> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 392" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:56> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "100"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "18"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "fd93de0b531f7b06b26a6a55c8dd136cbac2a641dd3fc8ac1611fa9ccfc04995"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """100 million for the Chief Credit Officer. Transactions exceeding these limits must be escalated to the next authority level, as
outlined in Section 3.
Aggregate exposure limits are applied to counterparties, industries, and geographic regions to prevent excessive concentration
risk. For example, the bank’s single counterparty exposure limit is set at 10% of its Tier 1 capital, while the aggregate exposure
to any single industry sector cannot exceed 20% of Tier 1 capital. Exposures approaching or exceeding 80% of these limits
must be escalated to the CCO for review, and any breaches must be reported to the Board of Directors within 48 hours. The
bank’s Risk Management Division (RMD) monitors aggregate exposures in real-time and generates automated alerts when
thresholds are approached.
In addition to exposure limits, the bank enforces risk-based thresholds for specific credit metrics. For corporate borrowers,
these include a maximum leverage ratio of 4.5x, a minimum interest coverage ratio of 2.0x, and a minimum current ratio of 1.2x.
For retail portfolios, thresholds include a maximum delinquency rate of 3% and a minimum FICO score of 650 for new
originations. Transactions or portfolios breaching these thresholds must be escalated according to the severity of the breach,
with more significant deviations requiring higher levels of approval.
The bank also applies concentration limits to specific asset classes and products. For example, exposures to commercial real
estate (CRE) cannot exceed 300% of the bank’s total risk-weighted assets, and within the CRE portfolio, no more than 15% of
the total CRE exposure can be concentrated in a single geographic market. Similarly, the bank limits its exposure to
speculative-grade (below BB-) counterparties to 5% of Tier 1 capital. Any transaction that would cause the bank to exceed
these concentration limits must be escalated to the CCO for exception approval.
Quantitative limits and thresholds are reviewed quarterly by the CPC, which may adjust them based on changes in the bank’s
risk appetite, capital position, or macroeconomic outlook. The CCO has the authority to impose temporary, more restrictive
limits in response to emerging risks or market dislocations. All changes to quantitative limits must be documented in the bank’s
policy management system and communicated to relevant stakeholders within 5 business days of approval."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.3"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:version:72> a cpi:PolicyVersion ;
    rdfs:label "Credit Duration Risk Policy v1.0" ;
    cpi:CONTAINS_SECTION <urn:cpi:section:51c8a785e8b8bf290468e926>,
        <urn:cpi:section:5a611c414915a832eb83a039>,
        <urn:cpi:section:6484f406a46e60be85a30569>,
        <urn:cpi:section:b4c626450515e2dca50d9558>,
        <urn:cpi:section:d7d69bc30d7131ba24e893ff>,
        <urn:cpi:section:e7987e65d75b692ff14fde1e>,
        <urn:cpi:section:eef0c39daac09fa21d0e26d1> ;
    cpi:HAS_EFFECTIVE_PERIOD <urn:cpi:version:72:effective-period> ;
    cpi:VERSION_OF <urn:cpi:policy:34> ;
    cpi:sourceRef <urn:cpi:source:72:1608> ;
    cpi:source_checksum "1dd66e05d6be0b8de2ae8d5020a930941c17fa515a794474c89f049c3098bae3"^^xsd:string ;
