<urn:cpi:source:48:230> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 230" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:48> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "10"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "f0f4e6260c627d43eb8b58e67793b57bde109ac6ae80977423b62fc82bad51e5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits and Thresholds
Quantitative limits and thresholds for coverage ratios are established to ensure consistency and discipline in the credit approval
process. The minimum DSCR threshold is set at 1.25x for standard commercial loans, with a higher threshold of 1.50x for loans
to borrowers in high-risk sectors such as real estate, hospitality, or start-up ventures. For investment-grade borrowers, the
minimum ICR threshold is 3.00x, while for speculative-grade borrowers, it is 4.00x. These thresholds are synthetic and
designed to illustrate the bank’s risk-based approach to underwriting.
The CCR threshold for secured lending is set at a minimum of 1.30x, with haircuts applied to collateral based on its liquidity and
volatility. For example, marketable securities are subject to a 10% haircut, while real estate and inventory are subject to a 20%
haircut. The policy also requires that collateral be revalued at least annually, with more frequent revaluations triggered by
material changes in market conditions or the borrower’s financial performance.
In addition to the minimum thresholds, the policy establishes warning and breach levels for each ratio. A DSCR between 1.00x
and 1.25x triggers a warning, requiring enhanced monitoring and a corrective action plan. A DSCR below 1.00x constitutes a
breach, requiring immediate escalation to the CCO and the development of a remediation plan. Similarly, an ICR between 2.00x
and 3.00x (for investment-grade borrowers) triggers a warning, while an ICR below 2.00x constitutes a breach.
The policy also includes sector-specific thresholds to account for the unique risks of different industries. For example, borrowers
in the oil and gas sector may be required to maintain a DSCR of 1.75x due to the volatility of commodity prices. Similarly,
borrowers in the technology sector may be subject to higher ICR thresholds to account for the uncertainty of future cash flows.
These sector-specific thresholds are reviewed annually by the CRC and adjusted as necessary.
This section of the synthetic policy highlights the importance of quantitative limits in managing credit risk. The thresholds and
limits outlined herein are illustrative and designed to demonstrate the bank’s approach to setting prudent yet flexible
underwriting standards. Actual thresholds may vary based on the bank’s risk appetite, regulatory requirements, and market
conditions."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "2.4"^^xsd:string .

<urn:cpi:source:21:127> a cpi:SourcePassage ;
    rdfs:label "Source clause 127" ;
    cpi:SOURCE_IN_VERSION <urn:cpi:version:21> ;
    cpi:clause_reference "5"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:document_reference "6"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:origin "POLICY_SOURCE"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:page 5 ;
    cpi:source_checksum "bf50dd9c0c4dccbbcf70a49edc56d70485262c9d6d116211909e092615d0e96f"^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:status "APPROVED" ;
    cpi:synthetic true ;
    cpi:text """5. 5. Quantitative Limits
The quantitative limits for LTV ceilings are structured to align with the bank’s risk appetite and regulatory requirements. These
limits are expressed as maximum percentages of the collateral’s appraised value and are tiered based on product type,
collateral type, and borrower characteristics. The following are the standard LTV ceilings for the bank’s retail lending products:
For primary residences, the maximum LTV is 80% for conventional loans, 90% for government-insured loans (e.g., FHA, VA),
and 75% for jumbo loans (loan amounts exceeding $1,000,000). For secondary or vacation homes, the maximum LTV is
reduced to 70% for conventional loans and 80% for government-insured loans. Investment properties are subject to a stricter
LTV ceiling of 70% for conventional loans and 75% for government-insured loans.
Auto loans are subject to a maximum LTV of 120% for new vehicles and 100% for used vehicles. The higher ceiling for new
vehicles accounts for the immediate depreciation upon purchase, while the lower ceiling for used vehicles reflects their higher
risk of rapid value decline. For secured personal loans, the maximum LTV is set at 70%, regardless of the collateral type, to
ensure a conservative approach to unsecured risk.
The bank also imposes sub-limits for specific scenarios. For example, cash-out refinances are limited to a maximum LTV of
75% for conventional loans and 85% for government-insured loans. Additionally, loans for properties located in high-risk
geographic areas (e.g., flood zones) may be subject to a 10% reduction in the standard LTV ceiling.
These quantitative limits are reviewed annually by the RCRC to ensure they remain aligned with the bank’s risk appetite, market
conditions, and regulatory changes. Any adjustments to the LTV ceilings must be approved by the RCRC and communicated to
all relevant stakeholders through the bank’s policy management system.
Underwriters are required to use the bank’s approved LTV calculation tools, which automatically apply the relevant ceilings
based on the loan product, collateral type, and borrower profile. Manual calculations are permitted only in exceptional
circumstances and must be documented and approved by a supervisor."""^^xsd:string ;
    cpi:version "3.1"^^xsd:string .

